WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.170
- Good evening everyone.

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:07.210
My name is MJ

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:09.540
and I'm part of the Migrant
Justice Initiative Project

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:10.730
at Yale University.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:12.490
I am so excited that you joined us

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:13.930
for today's book conversation

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:16.820
between author Karla Cornejo Villavicencio

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:18.970
and Professor Albert Laguna.

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:20.660
Before we introduce each other,

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:22.660
we'd like to acknowledge
the stolen Quinnipiac land

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:24.540
on which Yale university stands

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.470
as well as the indigenous
peoples and nations

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:28.630
that have historically cared
for and maintained the land

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:31.340
which we now call Connecticut,
including the Mohegan,

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:34.480
Mashantucket Pequot, Eastern
Pequot, Schaghticoke,

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:36.360
Golden Hill Paugussett, Niantic,

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.140
and other Algonquian speaking peoples.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.720
A little bit about the
Migrant Justice Initiative,

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:43.380
we are a multidisciplinary project

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.210
at the Yale Center for the Study of Race,

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.870
Indigeneity, and Transnational Migration

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:49.430
that aims to establish relationships

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:53.270
between scholars and migrant-led
social justice campaigns.

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:56.050
Our goal is to engage in
discussions of agency, mobility,

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:58.160
displacement, politics, and culture

00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.440
from the perspective of migrants

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:01.300
in order to better
understand their realities

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.170
and the structures that shape them.

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:04.350
Our team currently includes:

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:09.350
Alicia, Katy Maldonado, Ramon,
Maggie Katz and MJ, myself.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.250
And now I'd like to
introduce our moderator

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.190
and our speaker for today,

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:18.450
I'd like to begin with
Professor Albert Sergio Laguna,

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:21.440
he is an Associate Professor
of Ethnicity Race and Migration

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.760
and American Studies at Yale University.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.740
His research and teaching
interests include,

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:28.150
transnational Latinx
literatures and cultures,

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:30.680
comparative ethnic studies,
performance studies,

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:32.760
and popular culture studies.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:37.420
His work has appeared and is
forthcoming in Latino Studies,

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.860
The Journal of Latin
American Cultural Studies,

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:43.190
Latin American Research Review,
Contemporary Literature,

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:46.240
Cultural Critique, Diario
de Cuba, The Miami Herald,

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.030
The Washington Post, and on cnn.com.

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:50.237
Laguna's first book,

00:01:50.237 --> 00:01:53.260
"Diversión: Play and Popular
Culture in Cuban America"

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:54.490
won numerous awards including

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:57.490
the 2018 Peter C. Rollins Book Prize

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:01.430
from the Northeast Popular
American Culture Association.

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:04.127
An article from his current book project,

00:02:04.127 --> 00:02:06.910
"Edgardo Vega Yunqué
and the Comedy of Race,"

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:08.700
was awarded the L.S. Dembo Prize

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:09.660
from Contemporary Literature

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.340
for best article published
in that journal in 2018.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.020
And then we have author
Karla Cornejo Villavicencio,

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.120
who is an immigrant
from Brooklyn in Queens,

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.420
Harvard graduate, Yale PhD student

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:23.000
and a writer who has contributed
to the New York Times,

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.290
The New Republic, Vogue, Elle, Glamor,

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:28.540
This American Life and more.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.927
She is the author of The
National Book Award finalist,

00:02:30.927 --> 00:02:33.630
"The Undocumented
Americans," a book of essays

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:35.920
and the upcoming young
adult novel, "Dream Girl."

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:37.570
She lives in New Haven with her partner

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:39.720
and her very cute dog.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:42.930
And now I'd like to pass
it over to Professor Laguna

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:44.580
who will be moderating the panel.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:46.390
- Hello, everyone.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:49.760
I'd ask you how you're all doing

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:51.490
and you can maybe tell me
in the chat if you'd like.

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:53.030
Karla, more importantly,
how are you doing?

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:55.960
- I am okay, hello.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:57.680
- Good to see you. Good to see you.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.680
We had a little Frankie
Cameo there before the talk.

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:05.570
So I'm excited to have the whole kind of

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:08.003
crew there along with you.

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:13.120
So, you know, Karla, a
lot of people on this call

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:16.460
I'm sure have read this
book, but others have not.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:17.840
So I thought we could start

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.220
with just a really basic you bringing us

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:23.010
into the world of the
undocumented Americans.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:26.050
Why did you write this
book and what is it about?

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:27.583
What are you trying to do with it?

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:34.310
- I wrote this book as a response to

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:39.410
having grown up with
representations of immigrants

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:43.440
that I didn't feel were
true to my community

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:47.570
or my family growing up and I wrote it.

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:51.300
I began researching and writing it

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:54.510
the morning after Trump
won the presidency in 2016.

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:58.860
So it's very much like a snapshot in time

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:03.213
and I wanted to do something
different with the genre.

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:06.820
You know, I'd always, you know,

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:08.190
ever since you were like in high school,

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:10.580
one of the basic things you
learn in like English classes

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:14.140
that like, after like the A bomb

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:17.483
or after something like a world war,

00:04:18.626 --> 00:04:21.900
there is like a change
in schools of literature.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:25.170
And I felt that I didn't
want to wait around

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:26.683
to see what would happen,

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:31.220
you know, what would result
of the Trump presidency.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:34.760
Like I was a child when 9/11 happened

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:37.880
and I remember people were wondering like

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:40.530
what would the 9/11
novel look like, right?

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:43.020
And I don't think they've been great,

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:48.020
but I do remember having a sense that

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:50.210
obviously things would
be bad for immigrants

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:52.180
during the Trump administration,

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:55.640
but also having a sense of what those,

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:58.550
what those like literary
works would look like

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:02.203
and wanting to provide an alternative.

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:04.020
- Hmm.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:08.243
So, I mean, you're
providing an alternative to,

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:11.340
I guess what you imagined
the kind of fallout

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:12.610
has been of a Trump presidency,

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:14.600
the narratives around undocumented folks

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:19.600
but you're also providing an
important kind of intervention

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:22.040
in representations of the
undocumented immigrants

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:25.360
more broadly, I think,
in mainstream discourse.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:28.660
You have this great
line, which reminds me,

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:30.570
I don't need much of a
reminder how funny you are

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:31.530
in this book, right?

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:36.530
There's moments of really great
lines, cutting comic lines,

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:38.447
but you say,

00:05:38.447 --> 00:05:40.557
"You didn't want this
book to be a rueful tale

00:05:40.557 --> 00:05:43.327
"about a sickly Victorian
orphan with tuberculosis

00:05:43.327 --> 00:05:46.090
"that didn't have a
social security number."

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:47.950
So I'd love to hear a
little bit more about,

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:51.070
maybe like the biz side of this, right?

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:54.080
How the publishing industry in particular

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:58.780
sells the narrative of Latinx folks,

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:01.140
undocumented immigrants, more broadly,

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:02.740
first, second generation.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:06.150
How you see the book in
conversation with those demands

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:08.410
and also if you could just
say a little bit about

00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:11.780
now that the book has received
a lot of positive attention,

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:15.150
you know, what that experience
has been within the industry

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:19.060
as you continue to kind
of gain that visibility.

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:21.690
- I mean, can I just say, I
don't know who's on the call

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:24.003
because of the way this is set up.

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:28.810
(Karla laughs)

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:30.410
I'm feeling triggered right now

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:34.570
because I don't know how
many of the people here know,

00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:38.640
Albert was one of my dissertation advisors

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:41.520
and before then one of my professors.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:45.933
And he's known me since
I was like 21 or 22.

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:50.980
And even though on Instagram
I say I'm 26, I'm 31.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:58.700
So your tone, your teaching
tone to me is very,

00:07:02.812 --> 00:07:07.043
it just brings me back to when
I was like 23 and crying off,

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:12.900
(Karla laughing)

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:16.070
which I think in a lot of author
talks that doesn't happen.

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:19.270
So I'm going to (laughing),

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:22.363
I'm gonna try to answer your
questions without crying.

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:26.040
- Clarify first that, so
I don't get hate mail,

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:28.150
that it wasn't because I was
mean to you that you're crying

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:30.460
but because we were having
difficult conversations

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:31.947
or maybe I was mean to
you, you can clarify it.

00:07:31.947 --> 00:07:33.820
- No, no, because he's a mentor.

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:37.900
And so it was like a
mix of academic queries

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:42.220
and sharing texts and this
would be inspirational to you

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:44.210
and you might wanna look at this resource,

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:46.260
let's do outlines.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:48.060
I always benefit from outlines.

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:52.960
And also just like, my
20s were a time where

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:57.960
we're a decade long few state
and Albert was there for it.

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:03.140
So if there are any
national enquirers here,

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:07.903
Albert is willing to tell it all.

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:13.593
What was your question?

00:08:13.593 --> 00:08:15.080
- What was the question?

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:17.340
Yes, I'm interested in the biz, right?

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:19.790
'Cause we've talked about
the publishing industry

00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:21.820
kind of the narratives
they like to publish about

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:25.370
Latinx communities, undocumented
and not undocumented.

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:26.647
And just keen to hear a little bit about

00:08:26.647 --> 00:08:28.740
how this book is pushing back

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:30.270
in conversation with those demands.

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:32.220
And also, you know, because
this book has received

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:33.630
The National Book Award, right?

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:35.300
I mean, it's getting a lot of attention

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:38.040
and it's taking you
probably into conversations

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:40.310
that you haven't had
before in the industry.

00:08:40.310 --> 00:08:43.598
I'd like to hear just, you
know, your experience with that.

00:08:43.598 --> 00:08:44.431
- Okay.

00:08:46.310 --> 00:08:49.430
Well, it's, I mean, it's very interesting.

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:53.240
I received pushback at every single

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:55.430
step of trying to write this book

00:08:55.430 --> 00:08:58.193
and publishing it and promoting it.

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:04.030
You know, not from, there's
some stuff that I can't say

00:09:04.030 --> 00:09:06.720
because I'd like to
continue to make a living

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:08.890
throughout my life in publishing.

00:09:08.890 --> 00:09:13.270
But the original draft of my book was

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:16.290
at another imprint that has now closed

00:09:16.290 --> 00:09:20.020
and they rejected the book

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:22.680
because they called it
broken and unpublishable.

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:24.113
And it's this book.

00:09:28.071 --> 00:09:30.560
And I asked why, and I
wasn't given a reason.

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:32.610
I was told that I was
going to be given a letter

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:34.730
explaining everything.

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:37.690
And I wasn't, I was just
given the line edits.

00:09:37.690 --> 00:09:42.230
And the line edits were
typical edits that were like,

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:44.543
you might wanna move this clause here.

00:09:48.230 --> 00:09:50.250
I won't reveal what happened later

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:54.400
but my editor protected my book

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:57.900
and it ended up in the hands
of Christopher Jackson,

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:00.770
who was an extraordinary
editor at One World

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:04.770
who edits Ta-Nehisi
Coates, Ibram X. Kendi,

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:06.523
and he added with us.

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:13.110
He really understood my
voice but it took him a lot,

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:18.020
a lot of time to understand me,
you know, and to understand,

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:24.560
to understand that there
could be someone like me,

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:27.670
a dreamer like me, who was,

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:31.690
who had different kinds
of loyalties, you know?

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:36.460
And so while he supported my vision,

00:10:36.460 --> 00:10:39.130
he was a little bit confused by it

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:41.280
and probably had to do with me

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:43.953
having a difficulty
articulating my vision.

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:48.470
You know, for instance,

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:50.533
the magical realism that's in the book,

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:54.470
he thought that that
was going to make people

00:10:54.470 --> 00:10:57.050
not believe in the reporting of the book.

00:10:57.050 --> 00:11:00.530
And some of the reporting
is reporting that I break.

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:03.940
So like the stories of
the 9/11 cleanup workers

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:07.760
has been pretty under reported.

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:10.955
And he's like, "People
are not gonna believe that

00:11:10.955 --> 00:11:14.700
"if you're just like,
you're making things up."

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:17.440
And like I was telling
you earlier, Albert,

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:20.970
I thought Elián González
was rescued by the dolphins.

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:23.750
And I listened to a podcast recently

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:26.537
where they were talking about
Elián and they were like,

00:11:26.537 --> 00:11:27.697
"You know, these people who thought

00:11:27.697 --> 00:11:29.680
"that he was rescued by the dolphins."

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:34.500
And I was talking to my
partner who is a white woman.

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:37.770
And I was like, I mean, I thought that

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:39.900
until I listened to the podcast.

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:43.680
Like I knew that that
wasn't literally real

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:45.830
but it is a kinda real, I mean,

00:11:45.830 --> 00:11:47.960
in the sense that if a group of people,

00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:50.720
a large enough group of people believe it,

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:53.660
and if their belief leads to a conclusion

00:11:53.660 --> 00:11:57.600
that sustains them, then it is real.

00:11:57.600 --> 00:11:59.610
I mean, it's a kinda real.

00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:04.610
it's a kinda real that is, that has actual

00:12:04.820 --> 00:12:08.513
material consequences in
people's lives, right?

00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:13.010
And so the way I think
about magical realism

00:12:14.082 --> 00:12:15.220
is super specific.

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:20.220
And so I explained it and what
we ended up doing in the book

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:23.570
is every instance of magical realism

00:12:23.570 --> 00:12:25.713
ended up being a dream, you know?

00:12:27.035 --> 00:12:30.730
But you know, every copy
editors, I, you know,

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:33.740
I received a lot of support
in my imprint from my editor,

00:12:33.740 --> 00:12:36.380
but, you know, copy editors, proofreaders

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:40.097
would circle paragraphs
in my book and say,

00:12:40.097 --> 00:12:41.447
"Are you sure you wanna say this?

00:12:41.447 --> 00:12:42.990
"This is vulgar."

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:44.887
Or, "Are you sure you wanna say this?

00:12:44.887 --> 00:12:49.887
"This is like, this is, you
know, this is really violent."

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:52.587
Or, "I don't think you,

00:12:52.587 --> 00:12:55.010
"I don't think you wanna publish
something that says this."

00:12:55.010 --> 00:12:59.470
Where I was like revealing
that I was cruel to my brother

00:12:59.470 --> 00:13:04.470
or I was revealing
something dark but funny,

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:09.550
like a fantasy that I had
about drowning a toddler

00:13:09.550 --> 00:13:12.293
which was not real, but it was,

00:13:13.540 --> 00:13:16.250
it still revealed
something about my psyche.

00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:18.983
And so I just had to, there's a,

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:22.840
I don't know if college students do this

00:13:22.840 --> 00:13:26.920
but when you're in
editing, you just do stat

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:29.400
if you want to reject an edit.

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:34.400
And I was just, I take
edits, but when they're about

00:13:35.260 --> 00:13:38.510
tone that people think
that I shouldn't be using

00:13:38.510 --> 00:13:42.750
because as an immigrant I am
not entitled to that tone,

00:13:42.750 --> 00:13:46.213
I just wrote stat, stat, stat a lot.

00:13:47.320 --> 00:13:49.670
And then when it came up
to promoting the book,

00:13:50.550 --> 00:13:54.320
you know, the thing they wanted
to lead with was that I was

00:13:54.320 --> 00:13:57.210
a Harvard grad, one of the
first Harvard grads to go from,

00:13:57.210 --> 00:13:58.630
you know, and I was like,

00:13:58.630 --> 00:14:01.000
that doesn't position my
book as a literary book,

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:04.770
that positions that as a
dreamer memoir, which is not.

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:06.200
I don't talk about Harvard.

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:07.890
I don't talk about Yale.

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:09.090
I don't talk about any of that.

00:14:09.090 --> 00:14:13.340
Like it's, I understood
why they had to do it

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:16.750
but then I had to work behind the scenes

00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:19.440
to sabotage the marketing plans

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:24.200
and to reach out to individual
people, individual editors,

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:28.603
individual publications and
say, this is a literary book.

00:14:31.070 --> 00:14:34.210
- I have a ton of questions
about the literary aspect

00:14:34.210 --> 00:14:37.370
of this book that I wanna talk about,

00:14:37.370 --> 00:14:39.653
but I do wanna put some more,

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:46.783
some more of the focus on
the kind of nuts and bolts.

00:14:48.550 --> 00:14:51.440
And here I have a question about
this translation, you know,

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:54.480
'cause I was reading this
book the second time around

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:56.620
as a literary work, trying to understand

00:14:57.670 --> 00:14:59.180
the different devices at work.

00:14:59.180 --> 00:15:01.870
And just one word that
kept coming up in my head

00:15:01.870 --> 00:15:04.470
was translation, right?

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:05.390
And your comments right now

00:15:05.390 --> 00:15:08.300
make me think of translation again, right?

00:15:08.300 --> 00:15:10.180
How it works as a concept in the book.

00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:12.410
You talk about your beef with translation

00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:14.610
in the context of journalists,
and often how they have to,

00:15:14.610 --> 00:15:16.990
they fail to convey the complexity

00:15:16.990 --> 00:15:19.370
of the language of their
Spanish speaking interlocutors.

00:15:19.370 --> 00:15:22.060
You talk about translation
in the context of,

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:23.960
someone like Santiago at the worker center

00:15:23.960 --> 00:15:26.860
who refuses to translate a pastor's sunny

00:15:26.860 --> 00:15:28.650
liberal takes on America.

00:15:28.650 --> 00:15:31.320
Then there's your own
approach to translating

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:34.370
the words of the folks
you engage with, right?

00:15:34.370 --> 00:15:37.020
And translating your vision
for a broader readership.

00:15:37.870 --> 00:15:39.040
And then I also want you to talk about

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:40.290
the question of translation,

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:43.680
translating this book into
Spanish and how that's going,

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:46.330
if it's going and you know,
what your thoughts are about it.

00:15:46.330 --> 00:15:48.700
So if you could riff a
little bit about translation

00:15:48.700 --> 00:15:50.650
as a kind of concept in this book

00:15:50.650 --> 00:15:52.310
and your relationship to it,

00:15:52.310 --> 00:15:53.980
I think that'd be really interesting.

00:15:53.980 --> 00:15:57.090
- Yeah. I mean, I think
that I first became

00:15:57.090 --> 00:15:59.800
acquainted with translation

00:15:59.800 --> 00:16:02.330
as a narrative that white audiences

00:16:02.330 --> 00:16:04.600
like to hear immigrant children talk about

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:06.890
or children of immigrant talk about,

00:16:06.890 --> 00:16:09.510
like vis-a-vis our
relationship to our parents.

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:13.200
Like we translate for our
parents that makes us special

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:16.470
and that makes us worthy
of a special kind of pity

00:16:16.470 --> 00:16:20.170
but also a special kind of attention.

00:16:20.170 --> 00:16:22.690
And I'd like to like pull all,

00:16:22.690 --> 00:16:25.190
you know, children of immigrants
who've applied to schools

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:29.040
using translation as part of
their college entrance essay.

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:29.873
I did.

00:16:29.873 --> 00:16:31.760
I mean, I didn't write
about being undocumented

00:16:31.760 --> 00:16:35.243
but I wrote a short story,
which I wasn't asked to,

00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:40.930
where I focused on translation
and also about lying,

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:42.983
also about lying through translation.

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:48.277
And then it, you know,
dawned on me now that I'm 31

00:16:48.277 --> 00:16:50.810
and I'm working on my healing

00:16:50.810 --> 00:16:54.800
that translation from the
time that you were very young,

00:16:54.800 --> 00:16:57.622
from the time that you're five years old,

00:16:57.622 --> 00:16:58.930
not just basic translation

00:16:58.930 --> 00:17:02.650
but like writing your own absence notes,

00:17:02.650 --> 00:17:04.860
translating for your report cards,

00:17:04.860 --> 00:17:08.010
translating at your own
therapist appointments,

00:17:08.010 --> 00:17:12.330
translating at your mother's
gynecological appointments

00:17:12.330 --> 00:17:14.830
where you may hear things about your dad

00:17:14.830 --> 00:17:16.810
that you rather not know.

00:17:16.810 --> 00:17:20.150
That's all part of like
this parentification thing

00:17:20.150 --> 00:17:23.360
that children of immigrants go through

00:17:23.360 --> 00:17:26.810
which is not our parents' fault.

00:17:26.810 --> 00:17:30.120
It is part of the system
that we've fallen into

00:17:31.430 --> 00:17:35.160
that is this monster of migration.

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:38.880
And it is not blaming anyone

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:41.000
to acknowledge that migration

00:17:42.910 --> 00:17:47.160
where the powers that are
supposed to protect us

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:49.630
and create laws to guarantee

00:17:49.630 --> 00:17:54.550
that we have humane and dignified
lives have neglected us.

00:17:54.550 --> 00:17:56.663
And so that we, the children,

00:17:58.420 --> 00:18:01.990
often have to become parent
figures to our parents.

00:18:01.990 --> 00:18:04.800
And so translation is very fraught,

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:08.250
and it's not the romantic sweet thing that

00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:11.960
white admissions officers think it is.

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:15.363
And so, yeah, translation was
interesting to me in the book.

00:18:16.820 --> 00:18:21.800
It was interesting to me as a
writer because whenever I read

00:18:21.800 --> 00:18:23.463
pieces of journalism that have,

00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:29.550
like literally transliterated
Spanish, English to,

00:18:29.650 --> 00:18:34.423
Spanish to English, there's
no art in the translation.

00:18:35.370 --> 00:18:39.810
And so they just like use
like one word for one word

00:18:39.810 --> 00:18:43.320
and it's, they make us all sound like

00:18:43.320 --> 00:18:46.930
we have a kindergarten
level language capability

00:18:46.930 --> 00:18:50.960
with absolute no nuance and
absolute no sense of humor,

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:53.570
absolute no sense of interiority.

00:18:53.570 --> 00:18:56.990
And I'm like, that's not
how we sound like, you know?

00:18:56.990 --> 00:18:59.060
Because when you read
us in the Spanish paper,

00:18:59.060 --> 00:19:01.683
when you read us in the El Diario, like,

00:19:02.890 --> 00:19:07.040
we're shady, we're like sassy,
we have conspiracy theories.

00:19:07.040 --> 00:19:10.780
Like, why don't we sound like
that in the English paper?

00:19:10.780 --> 00:19:15.470
And so my approach to
translating in this book was,

00:19:15.470 --> 00:19:16.790
well, as a child of immigrants,

00:19:16.790 --> 00:19:18.660
my thing is translating, right?

00:19:18.660 --> 00:19:21.800
So I translated for my subjects

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:23.690
the way that I did for my parents.

00:19:23.690 --> 00:19:28.690
So in real time, in real time,

00:19:29.050 --> 00:19:32.590
and when it was time for me to,

00:19:32.590 --> 00:19:36.500
and I didn't use recorders
because this was like 2016

00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:38.853
and people, I did not wanna scare people.

00:19:41.220 --> 00:19:42.370
As like Edith Grossman

00:19:42.370 --> 00:19:45.430
who translates for like
García Márquez and stuff,

00:19:45.430 --> 00:19:47.630
who translated for
García Márquez and stuff.

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:52.070
You have like certain number of words

00:19:52.070 --> 00:19:53.840
that you could use to translate,

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:56.060
why choose the simplest word

00:19:56.060 --> 00:19:59.200
to make you sound like
what you think we are,

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:04.200
which is like the simplest
possible common person

00:20:05.270 --> 00:20:07.630
when you have these other
words that you could translate.

00:20:07.630 --> 00:20:10.140
So, because I'm not an actual translator

00:20:10.140 --> 00:20:13.280
or an actual journalist, I chose a word

00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:18.280
that conveyed what I thought
the person meant, felt like,

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:24.140
if they were angry, sort of
like the spirit of the person,

00:20:24.140 --> 00:20:26.040
I got to know my subjects really well.

00:20:28.150 --> 00:20:32.560
So when you hear, when you
see their text in my book,

00:20:32.560 --> 00:20:34.330
it sounds like you're talking to someone

00:20:34.330 --> 00:20:36.073
that you know in your language,

00:20:37.140 --> 00:20:38.420
and I didn't make any text up,

00:20:38.420 --> 00:20:40.110
I didn't make any dialogue up.

00:20:40.110 --> 00:20:41.100
It's their words.

00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:44.353
I just translated the way
that a poet would translate,

00:20:45.970 --> 00:20:50.230
you know, literary language,
which is, you do some rewriting

00:20:50.230 --> 00:20:55.230
but you don't add, you just
choose the most beautiful

00:20:55.240 --> 00:21:00.240
or the most evocative choices,
which I thought was a way of

00:21:00.470 --> 00:21:02.360
giving back some dignity to our community

00:21:02.360 --> 00:21:04.560
which has been infantilized.

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:07.170
And another thing, yes, my translation.

00:21:07.170 --> 00:21:10.040
So my book, it was written in English.

00:21:10.040 --> 00:21:13.260
My maternal language is Spanish,

00:21:13.260 --> 00:21:16.563
but the language I write
in and think in is English.

00:21:20.327 --> 00:21:22.650
I mean, I don't know if I can
talk about this but whatever.

00:21:22.650 --> 00:21:26.423
So like my book was going to
be translated into Spanish.

00:21:27.630 --> 00:21:31.543
And they hired a Latin American,

00:21:32.530 --> 00:21:35.180
a white Latin American translator

00:21:35.180 --> 00:21:36.390
for the Spanish translation.

00:21:36.390 --> 00:21:37.837
So I spoke to this person and I said,

00:21:37.837 --> 00:21:40.920
"My book has my philosophy
on translating."

00:21:40.920 --> 00:21:42.570
And she said, "I don't agree."

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:44.370
And I said, "Okay, you do you boo."

00:21:44.370 --> 00:21:48.800
But like, this is a very
clear voice in my book.

00:21:48.800 --> 00:21:50.620
Like, it's very voicey.

00:21:50.620 --> 00:21:55.620
And also my readers are
going to be like immigrants

00:21:57.160 --> 00:21:59.420
and people connected to immigrants.

00:21:59.420 --> 00:22:02.230
Like, I'm from New York, like I, you know.

00:22:02.230 --> 00:22:06.750
And so I got the translation
back and it's in this like,

00:22:06.750 --> 00:22:11.750
Sor Juana Inés de la Cruz,
aristocratic Spanish.

00:22:12.410 --> 00:22:17.410
Like, okay, so I'm at the Yale
American Studies PhD program.

00:22:17.880 --> 00:22:21.200
I applied to two American
Studies PhD programs in English,

00:22:21.200 --> 00:22:23.540
Harvard and Yale, everything
else I applied for

00:22:23.540 --> 00:22:25.720
was Spanish PhD programs

00:22:25.720 --> 00:22:28.920
or Latin American PhD programs in Spanish.

00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:30.580
Like, I get it.

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:31.413
I got it.

00:22:33.460 --> 00:22:35.210
The editors thought it was a class thing.

00:22:35.210 --> 00:22:39.030
He told me, like, we
can try to get this to

00:22:39.030 --> 00:22:40.967
sound more like street Spanish.

00:22:40.967 --> 00:22:43.487
And I was like, "Excuse me, sir,

00:22:43.487 --> 00:22:48.457
"do you know that Latinx Spanish
is a different vernacular

00:22:48.457 --> 00:22:51.907
"than the Latin American or
aristocratic Spanish, sir?"

00:22:53.083 --> 00:22:55.600
And then it just wasn't worth
my time because like I said,

00:22:55.600 --> 00:23:00.240
I'm on a healing journey and I
just don't waste that energy.

00:23:00.240 --> 00:23:02.297
Like Shakira says in that song,

00:23:02.297 --> 00:23:05.260
"You don't throw pearls
to the pigs," you know?

00:23:05.260 --> 00:23:07.590
I was not going to change his mind.

00:23:07.590 --> 00:23:10.763
My editor tried to speak
on my behalf and say,

00:23:11.627 --> 00:23:13.227
"Karla's right," like,

00:23:13.227 --> 00:23:15.431
"You have to get a different translator."

00:23:15.431 --> 00:23:16.264
Do you know what he said to me?

00:23:16.264 --> 00:23:19.050
The typical response of racist white men

00:23:19.050 --> 00:23:21.340
who say that there are
no qualified candidates.

00:23:21.340 --> 00:23:24.680
He said that he couldn't
find a Latinx translator,

00:23:24.680 --> 00:23:26.450
a qualified Latinx translator.

00:23:26.450 --> 00:23:31.450
And I said, "CUNY has a
program for translation."

00:23:34.175 --> 00:23:36.680
And then he was difficult
about it and he said no.

00:23:36.680 --> 00:23:39.040
So I pulled the translation

00:23:39.040 --> 00:23:41.200
and I just knew the book
was going to continue

00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:43.870
to be more successful and
I was gonna win more awards

00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:45.910
and I was going to have
my choice of translator

00:23:45.910 --> 00:23:47.630
at some point because like,

00:23:47.630 --> 00:23:51.890
what's the point of publishing
a book that's going to sound

00:23:53.450 --> 00:23:57.940
like an Argentine on ecstasy
masturbating to himself?

00:23:57.940 --> 00:24:02.940
This book is written by
and for Latino immigrants

00:24:03.610 --> 00:24:05.710
and immigrant adjacents.

00:24:05.710 --> 00:24:08.793
If it's not gonna sound like
that, then it doesn't exist.

00:24:11.820 --> 00:24:14.440
- Okay, I think we have the
title of your next book there

00:24:14.440 --> 00:24:16.140
in our next hard hitting articles.

00:24:17.580 --> 00:24:20.050
The reception of this
book, and, you know, this,

00:24:20.050 --> 00:24:21.840
it's actually really
interesting to hear that, right?

00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:25.190
'Cause it gives you a sense of,
you know, when you think of,

00:24:25.190 --> 00:24:27.710
how a book like yours
makes it through the world

00:24:27.710 --> 00:24:28.557
of the US publishing industry,

00:24:28.557 --> 00:24:30.290
you know, you ever think about

00:24:30.290 --> 00:24:32.410
the kind of translation to
the Spanish aspect of it.

00:24:32.410 --> 00:24:35.990
I think that's very telling
and perhaps not too surprising.

00:24:35.990 --> 00:24:38.950
You know, I'd really appreciate
that riff on translation.

00:24:38.950 --> 00:24:41.423
And it really just hits home, like how,

00:24:43.360 --> 00:24:45.860
how well you do the job
of translation, because,

00:24:45.860 --> 00:24:47.780
you know, the measure
for me for that is that

00:24:47.780 --> 00:24:51.500
I remember the people
distinctly that you talk about

00:24:51.500 --> 00:24:54.770
and you're speaking with
in your book, right?

00:24:54.770 --> 00:24:56.680
It's not just like a
mass of people, right,

00:24:56.680 --> 00:24:59.340
with different, I remember
names and like situations

00:24:59.340 --> 00:25:01.670
and that's because
you're giving us a person

00:25:01.670 --> 00:25:04.012
through their words, right?

00:25:04.012 --> 00:25:07.343
And that's because of your
skill as a translator, right?

00:25:09.250 --> 00:25:13.700
So, you know, I wanna
illuminate that a bit more,

00:25:13.700 --> 00:25:15.390
your relationships with these people

00:25:15.390 --> 00:25:17.410
'cause it's such a big part of the book.

00:25:17.410 --> 00:25:19.850
It's what makes the book the book, right?

00:25:19.850 --> 00:25:21.290
So I'd love for you to
say a bit more about

00:25:21.290 --> 00:25:24.100
how you made those connections,
develop that intimacy,

00:25:24.100 --> 00:25:28.310
but I really, I'm more interested
secretly, not so secretly,

00:25:28.310 --> 00:25:32.753
in the failures of connection
that you talk about.

00:25:33.599 --> 00:25:36.040
You talk about it in a couple
of different ways, right?

00:25:36.040 --> 00:25:37.190
I think it's really important

00:25:37.190 --> 00:25:38.970
you don't shy away from the fact that

00:25:38.970 --> 00:25:41.700
you failed multiple times, right,

00:25:41.700 --> 00:25:42.760
to connect with people, right?

00:25:42.760 --> 00:25:44.140
And I think those failures are,

00:25:44.140 --> 00:25:45.568
dare I say, generative, right?

00:25:45.568 --> 00:25:47.840
So I'd love to hear more about that,

00:25:47.840 --> 00:25:49.190
the relationships you cultivated

00:25:49.190 --> 00:25:50.510
and you couldn't cultivate, right?

00:25:50.510 --> 00:25:51.836
I think that's--

00:25:51.836 --> 00:25:53.730
(Karla chuckles)

00:25:53.730 --> 00:25:56.780
- Which relationships
are you speaking about?

00:25:56.780 --> 00:25:59.360
- Well, I always think about the,

00:25:59.360 --> 00:26:02.280
when you're in, is it
Cleveland and, or, no Flint,

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:05.800
when the students, you're
like, "Let's go to New York."

00:26:05.800 --> 00:26:06.900
And they just kinda like looked at you,

00:26:06.900 --> 00:26:07.960
and you're, the trouble you had

00:26:07.960 --> 00:26:12.960
connecting with some of
the teenagers, pre-teens--

00:26:14.046 --> 00:26:14.907
- That was, yeah.

00:26:14.907 --> 00:26:16.173
- Put in context.

00:26:17.800 --> 00:26:20.670
I mean, you know, you bring
a lot of commonalities

00:26:20.670 --> 00:26:23.080
and kind of common ground with
these people to connect with

00:26:23.080 --> 00:26:24.540
but there're also big differences, right?

00:26:24.540 --> 00:26:26.710
You're light trajectory and the
people you're speaking with.

00:26:26.710 --> 00:26:31.370
So yeah, I'd love to hear
about those relationships

00:26:31.370 --> 00:26:34.190
because they are core, right, to the book.

00:26:34.190 --> 00:26:35.080
- So that was very,

00:26:35.080 --> 00:26:37.300
the Flint chapter was very
painful for me to write

00:26:37.300 --> 00:26:42.300
because it's something that I,
that is unresolved within me.

00:26:43.410 --> 00:26:45.390
And I know I can't,

00:26:45.390 --> 00:26:49.380
I have no idea who's on this
call other than Alicia face

00:26:49.380 --> 00:26:52.613
and Steve's S, big S on the screen.

00:26:55.367 --> 00:26:57.937
I know a lot of you, you know,

00:26:57.937 --> 00:27:00.180
Latinx youth who are here at Yale

00:27:00.180 --> 00:27:02.540
might be able to relate to this but

00:27:03.450 --> 00:27:05.220
when you've heard from the,

00:27:05.220 --> 00:27:07.400
you know, from your earliest memories

00:27:07.400 --> 00:27:10.840
that the way of getting
out is through education.

00:27:10.840 --> 00:27:15.270
And it just happens
through strokes of luck

00:27:15.270 --> 00:27:18.430
and genetic accidents that
you have no control over

00:27:18.430 --> 00:27:19.970
and work ethic.

00:27:19.970 --> 00:27:21.480
But you also know that your like

00:27:21.480 --> 00:27:23.270
uncle has incredible work ethic

00:27:23.270 --> 00:27:27.200
and your undocumented aunt
has like great work ethic.

00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:29.650
And your cousin that works
at Walmart has great ethic

00:27:29.650 --> 00:27:30.483
and then you're like,

00:27:30.483 --> 00:27:32.470
well, it's not just the work ethic, right?

00:27:32.470 --> 00:27:34.800
And you understand that it's just like

00:27:34.800 --> 00:27:36.223
kind of Russian roulette.

00:27:38.069 --> 00:27:40.000
And you're like, okay, so I did this,

00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:41.963
I did this the right way.

00:27:46.687 --> 00:27:49.230
And then as you grow
older you realize that

00:27:49.230 --> 00:27:50.370
it changes a lot of things

00:27:50.370 --> 00:27:52.270
and it doesn't change a lot of things.

00:27:54.520 --> 00:27:56.930
You know, some like right-wing websites,

00:27:56.930 --> 00:28:00.610
like, you know, try to
take me on and be like,

00:28:00.610 --> 00:28:04.200
the way to fame and fortune
is to be an illegal alien.

00:28:04.200 --> 00:28:08.720
And it's like, well, I'm a queer icon now

00:28:08.720 --> 00:28:10.913
but I definitely don't have no fortune.

00:28:15.174 --> 00:28:19.197
I know that college is not
the answer for everybody,

00:28:20.550 --> 00:28:24.490
but I also have a knee-jerk reaction

00:28:24.490 --> 00:28:27.890
if a young person comes up
to me and ask for advice

00:28:27.890 --> 00:28:31.207
to say, go to four-year
school, go to four-year school.

00:28:33.560 --> 00:28:34.820
I can't not.

00:28:34.820 --> 00:28:38.240
And if a young person says

00:28:38.240 --> 00:28:40.640
that they wanna do something in art,

00:28:40.640 --> 00:28:42.880
I need to know what their art looks like.

00:28:42.880 --> 00:28:47.280
And I need to personally
evaluate if I think that they are

00:28:47.280 --> 00:28:50.340
going to be able to make money off of it.

00:28:50.340 --> 00:28:52.550
And if I don't think they are,

00:28:52.550 --> 00:28:55.707
I will break their fucking heart and say,

00:28:55.707 --> 00:28:58.317
"I think you can do this as a hobby

00:28:58.317 --> 00:29:02.070
"but I think you should go
into engineering," you know?

00:29:02.070 --> 00:29:05.010
And a lot of people, including my partner,

00:29:05.010 --> 00:29:06.423
think I'm a monster.

00:29:08.170 --> 00:29:10.010
She teaches in education studies.

00:29:10.010 --> 00:29:12.900
And so she, you know, she
has a lot of kids of color

00:29:12.900 --> 00:29:14.460
who wanna be teachers.

00:29:14.460 --> 00:29:18.397
And sometimes I get them
on the sly and I'm like,

00:29:18.397 --> 00:29:22.650
"Hey, have you thought about law school?"

00:29:22.650 --> 00:29:23.517
You know?

00:29:23.517 --> 00:29:26.040
(Karla laughs)

00:29:26.040 --> 00:29:28.040
And it's just because

00:29:28.040 --> 00:29:31.320
I know a lot of these kids
have to support their parents

00:29:31.320 --> 00:29:33.680
until they die.

00:29:33.680 --> 00:29:36.610
And I worry about how they're
going to be able to do that

00:29:36.610 --> 00:29:38.830
on a public school teacher salary,

00:29:38.830 --> 00:29:41.590
on an elementary school
public teacher salary.

00:29:41.590 --> 00:29:46.170
And it's not my business
but I also just worry.

00:29:46.170 --> 00:29:48.470
And I haven't solved it for myself

00:29:48.470 --> 00:29:51.403
because I took an arts career,

00:29:53.000 --> 00:29:55.710
I'm doing okay in terms of

00:29:57.510 --> 00:30:01.763
recognition, laurels but I'm
not making money off of it.

00:30:03.110 --> 00:30:05.760
But I've also been lucky
enough to support my parents

00:30:05.760 --> 00:30:07.133
throughout the pandemic.

00:30:08.932 --> 00:30:13.430
And that's, I sort of
feel like, like that is,

00:30:13.430 --> 00:30:15.020
you know, like the J-Lo movie, "Enough"

00:30:15.020 --> 00:30:17.320
where she was just like,
does all of the like,

00:30:21.940 --> 00:30:25.550
like all of the trainings to
get ready to beat her abuser.

00:30:25.550 --> 00:30:27.680
I was like, I did all of this

00:30:27.680 --> 00:30:29.560
to be able to support my parents

00:30:29.560 --> 00:30:32.060
'cause I knew a pandemic was coming

00:30:32.060 --> 00:30:34.200
and they needed to live off of me

00:30:34.200 --> 00:30:37.393
because otherwise they
would literally die.

00:30:39.250 --> 00:30:41.590
And I want all of these
children of immigrants

00:30:41.590 --> 00:30:44.450
who come to me asking
for advice or who don't,

00:30:44.450 --> 00:30:46.520
I want them to be able to have that.

00:30:46.520 --> 00:30:50.680
At the same time, I don't
want them to be unhappy

00:30:50.680 --> 00:30:53.623
and choose careers that kill their souls.

00:30:55.003 --> 00:30:58.473
So that's the Flint chapter where I try to

00:31:00.190 --> 00:31:02.727
reach out to young teens and say like,

00:31:02.727 --> 00:31:06.380
"Look at me, don't you
have big Broadway dreams?"

00:31:06.380 --> 00:31:10.037
And they're like, "No,
I mean, I wanna just,

00:31:10.037 --> 00:31:12.157
"that place has great tips

00:31:12.157 --> 00:31:15.170
"and they probably make
more money than I do."

00:31:15.170 --> 00:31:18.150
But then I, like, I'm
like really heavy handed

00:31:18.150 --> 00:31:20.597
and I'm like, "Have you
thought about health insurance?

00:31:20.597 --> 00:31:22.510
"Have you thought about savings?"

00:31:22.510 --> 00:31:24.920
And I just can't help but be that person

00:31:24.920 --> 00:31:27.300
because of my traumas.

00:31:27.300 --> 00:31:30.660
And I felt that the most
honest way to handle that

00:31:30.660 --> 00:31:33.460
instead of somehow pretending

00:31:33.460 --> 00:31:35.280
that I've solved that for myself

00:31:37.730 --> 00:31:41.628
is just being totally,
like we say, in Spanish,

00:31:41.628 --> 00:31:44.240
(speaks in Spanish), just being like,

00:31:44.240 --> 00:31:49.240
look, I'm a product of
programs, like prep for prep.

00:31:49.680 --> 00:31:52.880
And I'm a product of
knowing that I was hungry,

00:31:52.880 --> 00:31:54.920
went hungry as a child.

00:31:54.920 --> 00:31:59.730
And that I had this one
thing that I was good at

00:31:59.730 --> 00:32:02.220
that it turns out I could make money from.

00:32:02.220 --> 00:32:03.460
And now I want,

00:32:03.460 --> 00:32:06.380
it's sort of like I remember
Kendrick Lamar saying

00:32:06.380 --> 00:32:10.370
in an interview that he,
I mean, what he does now

00:32:10.370 --> 00:32:14.120
is that he hires a lot of
talented kids from Compton

00:32:14.120 --> 00:32:15.720
to work for him.

00:32:15.720 --> 00:32:18.480
And I don't know how that's
going, but he says that like

00:32:18.480 --> 00:32:21.120
the first time that he
was at a nice hotel,

00:32:21.120 --> 00:32:23.590
he wished that he could just like,

00:32:23.590 --> 00:32:26.100
like fly in all the kids from Compton

00:32:26.100 --> 00:32:28.410
and just put them in that one hotel.

00:32:28.410 --> 00:32:31.020
And I think its a lot of survivor's guilt

00:32:31.020 --> 00:32:33.810
and I think it's a lot of hoping that

00:32:33.810 --> 00:32:36.640
the magic spell that worked
for you even a little

00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:38.610
worked for everybody else.

00:32:38.610 --> 00:32:41.100
And I think a lot of
people struggle with it

00:32:41.100 --> 00:32:44.130
and I think that no
one wants an answer but

00:32:44.130 --> 00:32:47.773
acknowledging the struggle is
what a lot of people needed.

00:32:49.628 --> 00:32:52.397
- I think, I mean, it's
important for you to say that

00:32:52.397 --> 00:32:54.530
and say it out loud.

00:32:54.530 --> 00:32:56.130
My next question was
going to be about that

00:32:56.130 --> 00:32:58.065
tension between the,

00:32:58.065 --> 00:33:00.800
you know, the password for the kids in,

00:33:00.800 --> 00:33:01.920
outside of Cleveland, right?

00:33:01.920 --> 00:33:05.740
Study hard and your anxiety is
about giving that kind of the

00:33:05.740 --> 00:33:08.053
the advice about education but,

00:33:10.332 --> 00:33:11.730
I think it's important to hear

00:33:11.730 --> 00:33:13.810
because so much of the
story is follow your dreams,

00:33:13.810 --> 00:33:14.643
do what you love.

00:33:14.643 --> 00:33:17.950
But for a lot of people they can't,

00:33:17.950 --> 00:33:20.380
or it's not that easy
to make that decision.

00:33:20.380 --> 00:33:23.630
So to show that you're
wrestling with it, I think is,

00:33:23.630 --> 00:33:27.283
in the book is really, is
really important and productive.

00:33:28.550 --> 00:33:30.910
You know, I wanna move
now, looking at the clock,

00:33:30.910 --> 00:33:35.910
to the question of this book
as capital L literature, right?

00:33:38.370 --> 00:33:39.773
And art, right?

00:33:40.980 --> 00:33:44.890
Because I think that's such an
important part of this book,

00:33:44.890 --> 00:33:46.240
the contribution that it's making

00:33:46.240 --> 00:33:48.530
is that it will be the subject of many

00:33:48.530 --> 00:33:51.670
English PhD dissertations.

00:33:51.670 --> 00:33:53.230
- There's not enough there.

00:33:53.230 --> 00:33:54.538
(Karla laughing)

00:33:54.538 --> 00:33:55.830
- Oh yeah, oh yeah, don't worry.

00:33:55.830 --> 00:33:57.530
I'm already starting to assign it.

00:33:58.570 --> 00:34:01.750
The pipeline, you know, we're
working on that pipeline.

00:34:01.750 --> 00:34:05.660
Someone has to be the Karla
Cornejo Villavicencio chair of,

00:34:05.660 --> 00:34:07.373
of American studies somewhere.

00:34:07.373 --> 00:34:11.010
- Oh no, please don't, I'll
send a cease and desist.

00:34:11.010 --> 00:34:12.890
(Professor Laguna laughing)

00:34:12.890 --> 00:34:15.180
- Well, I think I just wanna take a line

00:34:15.180 --> 00:34:16.013
from your intro, right?

00:34:16.013 --> 00:34:17.410
And I wanna ask you to
unpack it a bit, right?

00:34:17.410 --> 00:34:21.137
Where you say, "When you are
an undocumented immigrant

00:34:21.137 --> 00:34:22.627
"with an undocumented family,

00:34:22.627 --> 00:34:24.287
"writing about undocumented immigrants,

00:34:24.287 --> 00:34:26.487
"and I can only speak
for myself and my ghosts,

00:34:26.487 --> 00:34:29.297
"it feels unethical to put it
on the drag of a journalist.

00:34:29.297 --> 00:34:31.637
"It is also painful to focus on the art

00:34:31.637 --> 00:34:35.960
"but impossible to process the
world is anything but art."

00:34:35.960 --> 00:34:37.330
So I just want you to just unpack,

00:34:37.330 --> 00:34:39.450
what does it mean to process this world

00:34:39.450 --> 00:34:40.840
that you're writing about as art?

00:34:40.840 --> 00:34:41.830
Why is it important?

00:34:41.830 --> 00:34:43.460
And if you can clarify even,

00:34:43.460 --> 00:34:46.790
what's the creative and
creative non-fiction, right,

00:34:46.790 --> 00:34:49.903
that you're kind of
harnessing in this narrative?

00:34:51.460 --> 00:34:54.840
- Well, I mean, for me it
was important to, you know,

00:34:54.840 --> 00:34:58.380
I studied under Kate Dudley at Yale.

00:34:58.380 --> 00:35:02.320
And so I had an understanding
of like ethnographic writing

00:35:02.320 --> 00:35:04.380
and even like ethnographic
creative writing

00:35:04.380 --> 00:35:05.763
and ethnographic fiction.

00:35:07.110 --> 00:35:11.340
So I referred to my subjects
of my subjects, but I also

00:35:11.340 --> 00:35:14.870
while I was writing my book,
I made a conscious decision

00:35:14.870 --> 00:35:17.300
to write about them as characters

00:35:17.300 --> 00:35:22.100
because I wanted them to be,
like really rich people who,

00:35:22.100 --> 00:35:26.700
you know, had weight.

00:35:26.700 --> 00:35:28.820
And so it's sort of like

00:35:28.820 --> 00:35:30.800
when you build a character from scratch

00:35:30.800 --> 00:35:32.340
and now that I'm writing a novel,

00:35:32.340 --> 00:35:36.990
it feels harder to do with a novel.

00:35:36.990 --> 00:35:39.620
But I wrote them as characters.

00:35:39.620 --> 00:35:44.110
I wanted them to hold up to scrutiny.

00:35:44.110 --> 00:35:47.740
And in order to do that, I
had to do a lot of interviews

00:35:47.740 --> 00:35:50.010
where I just didn't ask them,

00:35:50.010 --> 00:35:52.500
how many times did you cross the desert?

00:35:52.500 --> 00:35:55.220
Do you send remissions to your family?

00:35:55.220 --> 00:35:57.750
Like, what does it mean to be American?

00:35:57.750 --> 00:36:00.700
That's not how you write
a character, right?

00:36:00.700 --> 00:36:04.140
That's how you sort of
fill out a census form.

00:36:04.140 --> 00:36:07.380
So what I did was I just
talked to them about

00:36:07.380 --> 00:36:11.163
just really boring things and just,

00:36:12.840 --> 00:36:14.440
like not a lot happens in my book.

00:36:14.440 --> 00:36:16.849
There's not really much plot.

00:36:16.849 --> 00:36:18.020
Like one of the stories

00:36:18.020 --> 00:36:19.910
that people seem to respond to a lot

00:36:19.910 --> 00:36:24.510
and one of the loveliest
conversations I had was with,

00:36:24.510 --> 00:36:25.627
I forget the name I give him in the book

00:36:25.627 --> 00:36:28.670
'cause I changed everyone's names but

00:36:28.670 --> 00:36:32.030
a man in the Flint chapter who
worked at a chocolate factory

00:36:32.030 --> 00:36:34.110
and who had two pit bulls

00:36:34.110 --> 00:36:37.890
and that's all I say about him, really.

00:36:37.890 --> 00:36:41.010
He worked at a chocolate
factory and then he got fired

00:36:41.010 --> 00:36:45.900
and he gave his filtered
water to his two pit bulls.

00:36:45.900 --> 00:36:49.067
And usually in a story
where you have like,

00:36:49.067 --> 00:36:52.960
you know, a piece of art about immigrants,

00:36:52.960 --> 00:36:56.780
you want like a story, you
want there to be trauma,

00:36:56.780 --> 00:36:59.540
you want there to be action,
you want there to be,

00:36:59.540 --> 00:37:01.260
but then nothing happens here.

00:37:01.260 --> 00:37:02.830
It's just that.

00:37:02.830 --> 00:37:05.140
But the way that I was
able to write about him

00:37:05.140 --> 00:37:10.140
and sort of like the
slow sort of pace that

00:37:10.440 --> 00:37:13.633
people really responded to was that I,

00:37:15.760 --> 00:37:18.310
you know, I sort of built
him up like a paper doll.

00:37:21.853 --> 00:37:22.800
I cared about him

00:37:22.800 --> 00:37:24.530
and I think you can tell
that when you read the book

00:37:24.530 --> 00:37:27.800
and I thought what made
him so special was that

00:37:28.880 --> 00:37:30.990
people think about us as

00:37:30.990 --> 00:37:33.940
people who will do jobs
Americans won't do.

00:37:33.940 --> 00:37:37.120
We're just like really hard
workers or like whatever.

00:37:37.120 --> 00:37:39.480
But here's a man who
took like great delight

00:37:39.480 --> 00:37:41.920
and great dignity in having this really

00:37:43.060 --> 00:37:47.000
this job that he loved
with these fantastic smells

00:37:47.000 --> 00:37:48.370
and where at the end

00:37:48.370 --> 00:37:51.560
they thought that they
could infantilize him

00:37:51.560 --> 00:37:54.170
by giving him a certificate that was like,

00:37:54.170 --> 00:37:57.793
you did great, bud, when
they fired him for no reason.

00:37:58.734 --> 00:37:59.567
And the fact that

00:37:59.567 --> 00:38:01.480
when people think of
undocumented immigrants,

00:38:01.480 --> 00:38:02.700
they're not gonna think that

00:38:02.700 --> 00:38:05.380
someone loved his rescue
pit bull so much that

00:38:07.280 --> 00:38:10.460
they would give them
their own filtered water

00:38:10.460 --> 00:38:12.350
during a water crisis.

00:38:12.350 --> 00:38:13.460
No one's gonna think that.

00:38:13.460 --> 00:38:16.573
You know, that is such a
beautiful humanizing detail.

00:38:18.487 --> 00:38:20.867
And I thought that says so much,

00:38:20.867 --> 00:38:22.033
that says so much.

00:38:25.150 --> 00:38:28.010
Yeah, so it was, you know,
it was things like that

00:38:28.010 --> 00:38:30.910
that I think really build a character.

00:38:30.910 --> 00:38:34.150
So for me it wasn't about
representing subjects

00:38:34.150 --> 00:38:36.860
because representing subjects for me,

00:38:36.860 --> 00:38:40.500
I think could be just like,
you already have in your mind

00:38:40.500 --> 00:38:43.203
what you wanna say about
them, like hardworking,

00:38:45.143 --> 00:38:50.143
you know, family values, pays
his taxes, you know, whatever.

00:38:50.610 --> 00:38:53.163
But I wanted see what was,

00:38:56.630 --> 00:39:00.960
you know, what made that
person special and everything,

00:39:00.960 --> 00:39:04.030
you know, there's good special,
bad special, weird special

00:39:04.030 --> 00:39:06.620
but every person has a little something.

00:39:06.620 --> 00:39:10.280
And so I just treated people as characters

00:39:10.280 --> 00:39:13.160
and I think that's what made it creative.

00:39:13.160 --> 00:39:14.560
- Yeah, I think, you know,

00:39:14.560 --> 00:39:16.060
when you're talking about the smells

00:39:16.060 --> 00:39:19.370
since you've began
talking about this person,

00:39:19.370 --> 00:39:21.670
I was instantly transported to

00:39:21.670 --> 00:39:23.640
kind of sensory experience
of reading a text.

00:39:23.640 --> 00:39:28.640
I can almost smell the floor
of that candy factory, right?

00:39:29.750 --> 00:39:32.050
So again, effective.

00:39:32.050 --> 00:39:35.100
And I'm kind of, you
know, I want candy now.

00:39:35.100 --> 00:39:38.720
So if I can ask you to go a
little bit more on this kind of,

00:39:38.720 --> 00:39:40.370
this question of creativity, right?

00:39:40.370 --> 00:39:41.670
I wanna ask you about the moment

00:39:41.670 --> 00:39:44.810
that people always ask you about, right?

00:39:44.810 --> 00:39:46.160
The way you imagined

00:39:47.100 --> 00:39:51.030
the death of Ubaldo Cruz Martínez, right?

00:39:51.030 --> 00:39:55.900
'Cause I'm particularly
attracted to what you say on,

00:39:55.900 --> 00:39:59.500
after you relate his story
after drowning in a basement

00:40:00.844 --> 00:40:02.617
with Hurricane Sandy, you say,

00:40:02.617 --> 00:40:03.477
"What if this is how

00:40:03.477 --> 00:40:06.137
"in the face of so much
sacrilege and slander,

00:40:06.137 --> 00:40:07.590
"we reclaim our dead?"

00:40:07.590 --> 00:40:10.140
So the question of imagination and art,

00:40:10.140 --> 00:40:13.740
it's how I'm kind of
connecting the dots here.

00:40:13.740 --> 00:40:15.810
So can you say a little
bit about that moment

00:40:15.810 --> 00:40:18.840
because it is such a, I think
key moment for unpacking

00:40:18.840 --> 00:40:21.060
the way kind of creativity
is working in this text

00:40:21.060 --> 00:40:24.610
and how you think the work of creativity,

00:40:24.610 --> 00:40:29.270
why you think it's important?

00:40:29.270 --> 00:40:34.160
- You know, I just, he was,

00:40:34.160 --> 00:40:37.070
his is the only occasion in the book

00:40:37.070 --> 00:40:38.803
where I use someone's real name.

00:40:39.730 --> 00:40:42.393
I use pseudonyms for
everyone except for him.

00:40:44.680 --> 00:40:46.640
All of the daily laborers that I knew

00:40:46.640 --> 00:40:48.963
and I became very close to,

00:40:51.370 --> 00:40:53.543
the day laborers in Staten Island,

00:40:55.100 --> 00:40:57.940
most of them knew him or knew of him.

00:40:57.940 --> 00:41:01.590
And when I talked to them
about him, there was a sense of

00:41:02.430 --> 00:41:06.190
slight, I guess there was a lot of sadness

00:41:06.190 --> 00:41:10.660
with slight embarrassment
because of the way he died.

00:41:10.660 --> 00:41:15.660
And what was originally in
the book and I cut out was

00:41:16.320 --> 00:41:20.710
for no reason other than
space or just editing was that

00:41:22.580 --> 00:41:27.470
if I died right now, if I was
just in like a freak accident,

00:41:27.470 --> 00:41:32.470
slipped and hit my head,
because of who I am,

00:41:34.820 --> 00:41:39.120
which is just like a loud spoken person

00:41:39.120 --> 00:41:44.120
who is unapologetic
about being undocumented,

00:41:46.070 --> 00:41:50.490
an autopsy, or, you
know, the results of that

00:41:52.210 --> 00:41:54.493
would probably be made political.

00:41:56.610 --> 00:41:59.563
And what it would reveal is,

00:42:00.500 --> 00:42:04.550
like up for literary
interpretation, you know?

00:42:04.550 --> 00:42:08.620
I'm on a lot of medications
in order to keep me alive.

00:42:08.620 --> 00:42:12.660
And given the day, given
how hard things are,

00:42:12.660 --> 00:42:17.660
given how unstable or kind or
unboundaried my parents are,

00:42:18.710 --> 00:42:21.540
given the political news of the day,

00:42:21.540 --> 00:42:25.240
like beside from the medications I'm on

00:42:25.240 --> 00:42:30.240
which lay men don't have understanding of,

00:42:31.248 --> 00:42:33.680
and some of those medications
are scary-sounding

00:42:33.680 --> 00:42:35.473
like anti-psychotics, right?

00:42:36.420 --> 00:42:38.680
They don't know what they're treating.

00:42:38.680 --> 00:42:39.830
They're used off-label

00:42:39.830 --> 00:42:42.283
to treat my borderline
personality disorder.

00:42:44.000 --> 00:42:46.580
But there's also, you know,

00:42:46.580 --> 00:42:49.710
I might have a
benzodiazepine in my system.

00:42:49.710 --> 00:42:52.040
I might have taken a drink,

00:42:52.040 --> 00:42:54.050
I might have had a lot of kombucha

00:42:54.050 --> 00:42:56.990
which might represent for
some level of alcohol.

00:42:56.990 --> 00:42:59.150
You know, I might have
taken a beta blocker

00:42:59.150 --> 00:43:01.200
because that stops my panic attacks

00:43:01.200 --> 00:43:03.000
because it slows down my heart rate.

00:43:05.000 --> 00:43:08.290
You know, it might've been a day

00:43:08.290 --> 00:43:10.910
where I had a really
big deadline that night

00:43:10.910 --> 00:43:13.000
and I might've taken an Adderall.

00:43:13.000 --> 00:43:15.610
And if someone did a toxicology report,

00:43:15.610 --> 00:43:16.567
they would have said like,

00:43:16.567 --> 00:43:18.877
"Look at your little dreamer," you know?

00:43:18.877 --> 00:43:21.760
"Look at all of the substances she's on."

00:43:21.760 --> 00:43:26.120
And nobody deserves to
have a toxicology report

00:43:28.270 --> 00:43:32.400
or any phase in their
life define who they are

00:43:32.400 --> 00:43:35.970
or what their journey in
this country has been like.

00:43:35.970 --> 00:43:39.460
Because like Albert, you know
me, like I'm doing okay now

00:43:39.460 --> 00:43:42.360
but there have been times
in my 20s and during Yale

00:43:42.360 --> 00:43:43.490
where I was not doing okay

00:43:43.490 --> 00:43:45.700
and I did not handle my
mental health issues well.

00:43:45.700 --> 00:43:47.520
And my mental health issues

00:43:47.520 --> 00:43:49.860
were intimately tied to my immigration

00:43:49.860 --> 00:43:51.460
and the fact that I did not know

00:43:51.460 --> 00:43:53.470
they were tied to my immigration.

00:43:53.470 --> 00:43:55.640
And I was sort of walking around blindly

00:43:57.260 --> 00:44:00.080
and I did not receive culturally sensitive

00:44:00.080 --> 00:44:01.873
mental health treatment for them.

00:44:03.039 --> 00:44:07.140
And I was, you know, I fell
asleep in coffee shops.

00:44:07.140 --> 00:44:08.830
I passed out in places.

00:44:08.830 --> 00:44:12.400
I like vomited blood
outside of lecture halls.

00:44:12.400 --> 00:44:14.950
What if like that had been the moment

00:44:14.950 --> 00:44:17.210
where I had become public

00:44:17.210 --> 00:44:20.720
and that had been the name
people remembered me by?

00:44:20.720 --> 00:44:24.430
So once you have that in your head,

00:44:24.430 --> 00:44:29.400
you understand that
immigration affects us all

00:44:29.400 --> 00:44:31.490
as we are all immigrants,

00:44:31.490 --> 00:44:33.490
in the sense that we are all immigrants.

00:44:36.080 --> 00:44:38.850
And I didn't think it was
fair that that was his death

00:44:38.850 --> 00:44:43.850
because a lot of people
drink during storms.

00:44:45.900 --> 00:44:48.710
He was drinking during the hurricane.

00:44:48.710 --> 00:44:50.730
A lot of people drink during storms

00:44:50.730 --> 00:44:53.200
and it just happened that he died.

00:44:53.200 --> 00:44:57.170
And so I thought that as someone who has

00:44:57.170 --> 00:45:00.003
total power over what I put on the page,

00:45:01.210 --> 00:45:05.390
I could either report it the
way that newspapers reported it

00:45:05.390 --> 00:45:08.520
which was this alcoholic
daily laborer drowned

00:45:09.370 --> 00:45:13.230
'cause he was, you know, cause of vice

00:45:13.230 --> 00:45:16.630
or because of the understanding
that I had about the life

00:45:16.630 --> 00:45:21.340
that migration takes on a
body and on a mind and a soul,

00:45:21.340 --> 00:45:25.410
I could envision a
different ending for him.

00:45:25.410 --> 00:45:27.790
And it was a reclamation.

00:45:27.790 --> 00:45:31.810
And I felt that that reclamation
was for him personally

00:45:31.810 --> 00:45:36.810
because my heart dictated I
make it but it was also for me,

00:45:36.810 --> 00:45:39.400
it was also a reclamation for

00:45:39.400 --> 00:45:41.810
the different people that
have been in my life.

00:45:41.810 --> 00:45:46.810
And the kind of political
pinata I could have been

00:45:48.130 --> 00:45:49.620
at different points in my life

00:45:49.620 --> 00:45:51.393
or could still be, you know?

00:45:52.700 --> 00:45:55.890
In the end it was an artistic call

00:45:55.890 --> 00:45:58.320
for compassion and empathy

00:45:58.320 --> 00:46:03.100
and also a reminder to my
less sympathetic readers

00:46:03.100 --> 00:46:06.630
that so long as they're
holding my pages, I am god

00:46:06.630 --> 00:46:08.630
and I control what happens in this book.

00:46:12.320 --> 00:46:13.970
That's really great that, you know,

00:46:13.970 --> 00:46:18.490
now I'm gonna read that
scene with a new set of eyes.

00:46:18.490 --> 00:46:21.913
I really appreciate that explanation.

00:46:22.980 --> 00:46:26.230
And also just, you know,
that moment in the book,

00:46:26.230 --> 00:46:29.720
just, it's pushing back against

00:46:29.720 --> 00:46:32.370
so much of even the
literature in Latinx studies

00:46:32.370 --> 00:46:34.800
or the mainstream discourse which is,

00:46:34.800 --> 00:46:37.393
try to count the undocumented,
it's about policies,

00:46:38.365 --> 00:46:39.440
it's about labor, right?

00:46:39.440 --> 00:46:43.270
So like making a space for
art and the imagination,

00:46:43.270 --> 00:46:45.680
I think is so important to

00:46:45.680 --> 00:46:47.480
what I love about this book so much.

00:46:49.458 --> 00:46:52.313
You know, I have some, let's
see what time it is, 5:50.

00:46:53.890 --> 00:46:56.450
This is kind of a, it's just
a, it's kind of a me question.

00:46:56.450 --> 00:46:58.070
I just really wanna know.

00:46:58.070 --> 00:47:00.663
Writing this book was clearly not easy.

00:47:01.620 --> 00:47:04.190
So I'm really curious just because

00:47:05.360 --> 00:47:06.770
I wanna get in your
head a little bit here,

00:47:06.770 --> 00:47:08.970
but what were you listening to and reading

00:47:08.970 --> 00:47:13.483
while you were working through
this, I'm very curious.

00:47:16.700 --> 00:47:19.340
- At the beginning, I read
a lot of James Baldwin

00:47:19.340 --> 00:47:24.110
to muster up the courage
to write this because I,

00:47:24.990 --> 00:47:27.270
I mean, I love his writing obviously,

00:47:27.270 --> 00:47:31.330
but I also knew that he had
a reluctance to write about

00:47:31.330 --> 00:47:35.180
some of the things he did
because it affected him immensely

00:47:35.180 --> 00:47:38.640
and he felt that it was an
obligation to give back,

00:47:38.640 --> 00:47:40.883
as he said, to the people who made him,

00:47:41.990 --> 00:47:43.860
to the people who he owed.

00:47:43.860 --> 00:47:46.053
And so I read a lot of him.

00:47:47.210 --> 00:47:50.110
And then I mostly stopped reading.

00:47:50.110 --> 00:47:55.110
I read some Jenny Diski because
I liked her voice but I,

00:47:56.760 --> 00:48:00.160
and I read a lot of Eileen
Myles who became a friend.

00:48:00.160 --> 00:48:02.233
And I love Eileen's,

00:48:04.450 --> 00:48:05.550
I love Eileen's voice.

00:48:05.550 --> 00:48:08.450
And there's sort of nobody who can,

00:48:08.450 --> 00:48:12.200
nobody's books who I read and then go,

00:48:12.200 --> 00:48:16.580
I really wanna right now,
you know, other than Eileen.

00:48:16.580 --> 00:48:18.300
But mostly I listened to a lot of music.

00:48:18.300 --> 00:48:20.780
And I think you can sort of tell

00:48:20.780 --> 00:48:22.380
with the cadence of my sentences

00:48:22.380 --> 00:48:25.690
that I was going for something that was

00:48:25.690 --> 00:48:29.090
kind of disjointed and chaotic

00:48:29.090 --> 00:48:30.950
but also really tightly controlled,

00:48:30.950 --> 00:48:34.490
something that was like written
and then played chaotically.

00:48:34.490 --> 00:48:39.490
And so I listened to a lot
of like The White Stripes

00:48:40.130 --> 00:48:42.600
and I listened to a lot of Fiona Apple

00:48:43.990 --> 00:48:48.990
and I listened to a lot of
Nirvana and the Arctic Monkeys.

00:48:50.910 --> 00:48:54.010
And it's so funny, the
Arctic Monkeys have,

00:48:54.010 --> 00:48:55.940
their songwriter, Alex Turner,

00:48:55.940 --> 00:49:00.720
he writes some really weird
lyrics that if I try to write

00:49:00.720 --> 00:49:02.870
would totally get edited out.

00:49:02.870 --> 00:49:04.330
But if I can say is that

00:49:04.330 --> 00:49:06.930
like everything that I tried to edit out,

00:49:06.930 --> 00:49:09.430
everything that anyone tried
to edit out of this book,

00:49:09.430 --> 00:49:12.050
any sentence, and that I fought for

00:49:12.050 --> 00:49:15.982
have now become my reader's
favorite sentences or passages.

00:49:15.982 --> 00:49:18.000
- Hmm, Hmm.

00:49:18.000 --> 00:49:21.140
That's, now I have a soundtrack
for when I read the book.

00:49:21.140 --> 00:49:23.190
I appreciate that, I'm taking notes here.

00:49:25.460 --> 00:49:26.740
Maybe two or three more questions

00:49:26.740 --> 00:49:29.610
before we open up to the Q&amp;A.

00:49:29.610 --> 00:49:32.170
I'm gonna ask you, ha
ha, kind of fiendishly

00:49:33.540 --> 00:49:37.530
to go back to your TF days, right?

00:49:37.530 --> 00:49:41.370
I'm curious about how
would you teach this book

00:49:41.370 --> 00:49:44.840
or if you don't wanna answer
that question, you know,

00:49:44.840 --> 00:49:47.290
maybe the question is,
you know, what do you want

00:49:47.290 --> 00:49:49.350
readers in college maybe
to get out of this book?

00:49:49.350 --> 00:49:50.820
But if you wanna put on the TF hat,

00:49:50.820 --> 00:49:54.130
you know, you're more
than welcome to do that.

00:49:54.130 --> 00:49:59.130
- Like I have no recollection
of my time as a TF.

00:49:59.600 --> 00:50:03.130
And sometimes like students,
I mean, they've graduated

00:50:03.130 --> 00:50:05.190
but like they would have been like, "Hey."

00:50:05.190 --> 00:50:08.983
And I'd be like, "Hey," I
did not remember any of them.

00:50:12.180 --> 00:50:13.363
You know what's crazy?

00:50:14.620 --> 00:50:19.060
I have gotten a lot of
messages from young people,

00:50:19.060 --> 00:50:23.120
college students who
are saying that they are

00:50:24.540 --> 00:50:28.570
comfortable experimenting
with the essay form

00:50:28.570 --> 00:50:30.440
and with first person writing

00:50:30.440 --> 00:50:35.020
and like that's based on
heavy research and interviews

00:50:35.020 --> 00:50:37.890
and that their professors
are actually encouraging them

00:50:37.890 --> 00:50:42.890
to do creative projects and
personal essays based on

00:50:43.600 --> 00:50:48.393
a lot of interviews and
"Reportage" and research.

00:50:49.360 --> 00:50:53.280
And all of these kids who
write me are kids of color.

00:50:53.280 --> 00:50:56.270
And I think that that's fantastic.

00:50:56.270 --> 00:51:01.030
I have like throughout
high school until now

00:51:01.030 --> 00:51:05.943
I have forced that to be the
case for me in all my classes.

00:51:06.850 --> 00:51:10.590
But the fact that I think,

00:51:10.590 --> 00:51:14.500
you know, I have a friend
who's an immigration journalist

00:51:14.500 --> 00:51:16.110
and she just got her first book deal.

00:51:16.110 --> 00:51:20.060
And she said that her editor
told her, like cited my book

00:51:20.060 --> 00:51:22.950
and said that she was allowed
to write in the first person

00:51:22.950 --> 00:51:26.133
which before, as a journalist,
you were not allowed to do.

00:51:27.490 --> 00:51:32.020
And so I think that, even
though my book is still not like

00:51:32.020 --> 00:51:35.550
huge, you know, it's been
really a grassroots support

00:51:35.550 --> 00:51:38.810
of a lot of Latinx people and a lot of

00:51:38.810 --> 00:51:40.380
immigrants and children of immigrants

00:51:40.380 --> 00:51:43.053
and people of color
who supported the book,

00:51:44.640 --> 00:51:48.040
I think that a lot of people
who are assigning the book

00:51:48.040 --> 00:51:52.240
or in the publishing industry have,

00:51:52.240 --> 00:51:53.710
I don't know why it took them so long,

00:51:53.710 --> 00:51:57.330
but have noticed that this
is something you can do

00:51:57.330 --> 00:52:02.330
and are validating the
genre of the personal essay

00:52:03.610 --> 00:52:08.610
that's based on reporting
and interviews and,

00:52:09.410 --> 00:52:11.090
you know, pop culture references

00:52:11.090 --> 00:52:14.070
and all of this genre that I worked with.

00:52:14.070 --> 00:52:19.070
And they, you know, it's kind
of like Dave Eggers released

00:52:19.357 --> 00:52:21.180
"A Heartbreaking Work
of Staggering Genius"

00:52:21.180 --> 00:52:22.370
and they were like

00:52:22.370 --> 00:52:25.160
a lot of Dave Eggers is
after that, you know?

00:52:25.160 --> 00:52:26.500
I was one of them.

00:52:30.121 --> 00:52:31.490
- So in the future when I teach your book,

00:52:31.490 --> 00:52:33.850
I'll send you my students' comments

00:52:33.850 --> 00:52:35.660
and, you know, reader's responses

00:52:35.660 --> 00:52:39.213
so you can be in the kind
of room as a TF, right?

00:52:40.150 --> 00:52:42.470
But, you know, you've mentioned
a little bit about your,

00:52:42.470 --> 00:52:45.020
the response that the book has received,

00:52:45.020 --> 00:52:48.240
your direct messages
about students feeling

00:52:48.240 --> 00:52:50.100
an ability to experiment
with form and genre.

00:52:50.100 --> 00:52:52.920
Can you say a little bit more
about that grassroots support

00:52:52.920 --> 00:52:57.493
and is there responses
from folks you're getting?

00:52:58.620 --> 00:53:01.030
- Yeah, I mean, at
first I was the only one

00:53:01.030 --> 00:53:04.743
who bet on this book, like
nobody believed in this book.

00:53:06.040 --> 00:53:09.350
My editor loved my voice, but
I don't think he understood

00:53:10.923 --> 00:53:13.420
that this book was as special as it was.

00:53:13.420 --> 00:53:18.420
It was entirely Latinx
readers on social media

00:53:19.240 --> 00:53:23.130
who discovered this book and
not just like young people

00:53:23.130 --> 00:53:26.640
in their college chucking
colleges but it was like

00:53:26.640 --> 00:53:31.393
from college age to like
children, like older,

00:53:32.600 --> 00:53:35.790
mature, older children
of like the original

00:53:35.790 --> 00:53:38.000
(speaks in Spanish) who were like,

00:53:38.000 --> 00:53:41.380
I've been waiting my entire
life to see a book like this.

00:53:41.380 --> 00:53:43.240
Who were really emotional and would like

00:53:43.240 --> 00:53:46.783
send me videos of them unboxing my book.

00:53:48.290 --> 00:53:51.250
And then like the younger
kids would be like

00:53:51.250 --> 00:53:54.240
sending videos of them
twerking to my book.

00:53:54.240 --> 00:53:58.550
And it was, I mean, it was really amazing.

00:53:58.550 --> 00:54:03.550
I got messages from black
immigrants, African immigrants,

00:54:03.630 --> 00:54:08.200
who just felt like they
didn't really belong in

00:54:08.200 --> 00:54:13.160
the Latinx activist movement
because of its politics

00:54:13.160 --> 00:54:16.030
that I haven't really aligned myself with.

00:54:16.030 --> 00:54:18.060
And they felt a little bit excluded

00:54:18.060 --> 00:54:20.120
from the Black Lives Matter movement.

00:54:20.120 --> 00:54:22.930
And they really found a home in my book.

00:54:22.930 --> 00:54:27.310
And I've gotten a lot
of messages from like

00:54:28.570 --> 00:54:31.920
children of Asian immigrants
who really loved the book.

00:54:31.920 --> 00:54:36.780
I've gotten messages from disabled readers

00:54:36.780 --> 00:54:41.780
who have felt comfortable
with the fact that

00:54:42.760 --> 00:54:47.250
so much of what makes someone
documented, not documented,

00:54:47.250 --> 00:54:50.620
a citizen, not citizen, smart,
not smart, good at SATs,

00:54:50.620 --> 00:54:53.330
all of this is luck and they understand

00:54:53.330 --> 00:54:56.270
the role that luck can
play in a person's life

00:54:56.270 --> 00:54:58.020
and the way that like can look,

00:54:58.020 --> 00:55:00.510
can determine whether or not
you're discriminated against

00:55:00.510 --> 00:55:02.720
or not for the rest of your life.

00:55:02.720 --> 00:55:07.133
And I, you know, I've had a lot of,

00:55:09.149 --> 00:55:13.510
you know, really successful
Latinx children of immigrants

00:55:15.350 --> 00:55:20.350
understand my brain and share
it and share that with me.

00:55:23.010 --> 00:55:26.860
And also, you know, I'm
very open on Instagram

00:55:26.860 --> 00:55:31.860
about my mental illness
and how hard work it is

00:55:33.170 --> 00:55:38.170
to stay high functioning
and stable and to,

00:55:41.090 --> 00:55:43.860
how sometimes we judge whether
or not we're doing okay

00:55:43.860 --> 00:55:46.110
with our mental health
by how productive we are.

00:55:46.110 --> 00:55:49.820
But I also try to make sure that I find

00:55:49.820 --> 00:55:51.910
some space for joy every day.

00:55:51.910 --> 00:55:56.030
And I've had a huge response
of Latinx kids being like,

00:55:56.030 --> 00:55:59.400
I am not doing well and I,

00:55:59.400 --> 00:56:01.180
you know, want to work on my mental health

00:56:01.180 --> 00:56:02.780
and that's meant the most to me.

00:56:05.690 --> 00:56:06.960
- Thank you for that.

00:56:06.960 --> 00:56:10.330
My last question before
we get to the Q&amp;A is just,

00:56:10.330 --> 00:56:11.950
you know, what's the next thing?

00:56:11.950 --> 00:56:15.083
You know, you teased it a
little bit, sounds like a novel.

00:56:16.283 --> 00:56:17.580
I'd love to hear a little
bit more about that.

00:56:17.580 --> 00:56:21.090
And maybe what, you know, to
focus on the art again, right?

00:56:21.090 --> 00:56:23.850
That shift from the undocumented Americans

00:56:24.991 --> 00:56:27.730
to the novel as a form.

00:56:27.730 --> 00:56:29.530
I'd love to hear about that as well.

00:56:30.560 --> 00:56:33.240
- Yeah, the novel, it's
a young adult novel

00:56:34.310 --> 00:56:39.310
and it's sort of loosely
based on my teenage years

00:56:39.660 --> 00:56:42.950
in New York city, writing
about music as a teenager

00:56:42.950 --> 00:56:45.610
and like being in that underground,

00:56:45.610 --> 00:56:49.730
the undocumented underground and the like,

00:56:49.730 --> 00:56:52.590
you know, multiple
undergrounds in New York.

00:56:52.590 --> 00:56:55.430
And it's like a very fast paced, gritty,

00:56:55.430 --> 00:56:57.563
pre-gentrified New York book.

00:57:00.199 --> 00:57:02.070
It's as if a beatnik

00:57:02.070 --> 00:57:07.070
was a queer, undocumented
Jehovah's Witness girl in Brooklyn

00:57:08.720 --> 00:57:13.620
who was rescued by a public
masturbator, by a drag queen

00:57:13.620 --> 00:57:16.850
before her Jehovah's Witness
dad picked her up at midnight

00:57:16.850 --> 00:57:20.680
at a jazz club in the village,
like that's the novel.

00:57:20.680 --> 00:57:24.203
And then after that, I have
a book of essays on cults.

00:57:27.100 --> 00:57:28.580
- Cults, spring, period.

00:57:28.580 --> 00:57:29.648
All right.

00:57:29.648 --> 00:57:33.460
(Professor Laguna laughs)

00:57:33.460 --> 00:57:34.510
Yes, Q&amp;A.

00:57:34.510 --> 00:57:36.900
Let's go to the Q&amp;A.

00:57:36.900 --> 00:57:40.410
The first question is, one attending notes

00:57:40.410 --> 00:57:42.830
your interest in shifting the
spotlight from the dreamer

00:57:42.830 --> 00:57:45.030
and onto the everyday
undocumented immigrant.

00:57:45.030 --> 00:57:47.280
Can you say more about how we
might continue these efforts

00:57:47.280 --> 00:57:49.773
on a nationwide political level?

00:57:50.620 --> 00:57:53.980
- I have no policy recommendations.

00:57:53.980 --> 00:57:56.430
(Karla and Professor Laguna laughing)

00:57:56.430 --> 00:57:57.683
I mean, I don't know.

00:57:59.880 --> 00:58:01.360
If you're genuinely interested,

00:58:01.360 --> 00:58:05.580
if you were like someone who
works on in policy spaces

00:58:05.580 --> 00:58:08.390
or like in activism spaces,

00:58:08.390 --> 00:58:11.370
you can contact Jose Antonio Vargas.

00:58:11.370 --> 00:58:14.630
He does that sort of how to get from

00:58:14.630 --> 00:58:18.150
hearts and minds to politics.

00:58:18.150 --> 00:58:19.173
I don't do that.

00:58:20.460 --> 00:58:21.900
Sorry.

00:58:21.900 --> 00:58:23.033
- Well, what you do do is so important,

00:58:23.033 --> 00:58:24.854
is that you're giving people,

00:58:24.854 --> 00:58:26.540
you know, they're gonna be people,

00:58:26.540 --> 00:58:29.570
you know, in 15, 10, 15
years probably even sooner,

00:58:29.570 --> 00:58:31.820
like, you know, I had my Karla stage

00:58:31.820 --> 00:58:33.060
where the undocumented Americans

00:58:33.060 --> 00:58:35.040
is really important for me, right?

00:58:35.040 --> 00:58:37.390
You're opening up a space
for people to imagine

00:58:39.030 --> 00:58:41.167
these communities, right,
in different ways, right?

00:58:41.167 --> 00:58:46.167
And I think that's perhaps
not, that's not a policy memo,

00:58:46.190 --> 00:58:48.780
but it is extremely
important as, you know,

00:58:48.780 --> 00:58:51.580
as people in the humanities
at least like to think.

00:58:51.580 --> 00:58:54.140
I include myself among them.

00:58:54.140 --> 00:58:56.563
Okay, so more questions.

00:58:57.690 --> 00:59:00.390
Can you say more about the
cover image of the book?

00:59:00.390 --> 00:59:02.123
This is from Laura Wexler.

00:59:04.620 --> 00:59:05.703
- Hi, Laura Wexler.

00:59:07.480 --> 00:59:08.313
Yes.

00:59:10.368 --> 00:59:12.623
The updated cover image of the book is,

00:59:13.570 --> 00:59:16.853
oh, I have it here so everyone can see it.

00:59:19.380 --> 00:59:20.813
It's this flower.

00:59:21.650 --> 00:59:25.920
And here are the USCIS documents,

00:59:25.920 --> 00:59:29.420
USCIS which is being
bankrupted intentionally,

00:59:29.420 --> 00:59:31.680
Stephen Miller brainchild.

00:59:31.680 --> 00:59:33.440
And then there are bloodstains

00:59:33.440 --> 00:59:35.990
which I requested to be all around there.

00:59:35.990 --> 00:59:40.700
And so I didn't have any control

00:59:40.700 --> 00:59:42.580
over why that flower was there.

00:59:42.580 --> 00:59:44.130
The flower was always there.

00:59:44.130 --> 00:59:45.703
And I was like, okay.

00:59:46.940 --> 00:59:51.940
And I then came up with the
explanation that it was like,

00:59:52.160 --> 00:59:55.740
like I've always written
about animals and wildlife

00:59:55.740 --> 00:59:58.670
and inhospitable places
like the Sonoran Desert

00:59:58.670 --> 01:00:01.010
or under like the deepest
fathoms of the sea

01:00:01.010 --> 01:00:02.650
and compared undocumented people to that

01:00:02.650 --> 01:00:05.660
because we survive in
inhospitable climates

01:00:05.660 --> 01:00:07.130
that want us dead, right?

01:00:07.130 --> 01:00:09.580
So I think that's what they
derived inspiration from.

01:00:09.580 --> 01:00:13.180
And then I wanted the
papers thing with the blood

01:00:13.180 --> 01:00:16.020
because as someone
who's gone through DACA,

01:00:16.020 --> 01:00:18.980
got a temporary green card that expired,

01:00:18.980 --> 01:00:21.500
I think a lot of people
who've experienced,

01:00:21.500 --> 01:00:24.620
who've been undocumented and
have experienced legalization

01:00:24.620 --> 01:00:26.620
or who have been citizens

01:00:26.620 --> 01:00:29.760
and then had to petition
their parents for legalization

01:00:29.760 --> 01:00:31.390
and then now in this era

01:00:31.390 --> 01:00:33.723
has seen that fall through all of that,

01:00:34.970 --> 01:00:37.740
there's a lot of guilt, there's
a lot of survivor's guilt,

01:00:37.740 --> 01:00:39.210
there's a lot of guilt.

01:00:39.210 --> 01:00:42.270
And you always feel like you
have blood on your hands.

01:00:42.270 --> 01:00:45.190
And I wanted to acknowledge the fact that

01:00:45.190 --> 01:00:48.750
one, immigrants are world makers.

01:00:48.750 --> 01:00:52.200
We make worlds out of thin air.

01:00:52.200 --> 01:00:54.840
And two, the idea of papers,

01:00:54.840 --> 01:00:57.380
having papers is always in the background.

01:00:57.380 --> 01:01:01.170
But even when you have
it, it never goes away.

01:01:01.170 --> 01:01:04.000
The trauma doesn't go
away and the feeling that

01:01:04.000 --> 01:01:06.100
you've left people behind doesn't go away.

01:01:09.250 --> 01:01:13.760
- So another question, what
are the things you do for joy?

01:01:13.760 --> 01:01:16.460
How do you find joy when
things feel harder than ever?

01:01:19.650 --> 01:01:21.450
- You know, it's a funny thing.

01:01:21.450 --> 01:01:22.860
This is, well, Albert, you don't know this

01:01:22.860 --> 01:01:27.300
but when I was Albert's grad
student, I was like 23 or 24,

01:01:27.300 --> 01:01:30.520
I used to go to his office,
clearly looking a mess.

01:01:30.520 --> 01:01:34.930
And Albert would like, like slide over

01:01:34.930 --> 01:01:39.930
a Ziploc bag of calming herbal tea to me

01:01:41.475 --> 01:01:43.925
and be like, "I think you
should take this home."

01:01:45.630 --> 01:01:47.360
Okay, so one of the
things that I do now is

01:01:47.360 --> 01:01:49.810
I try to find like sensory things

01:01:49.810 --> 01:01:52.420
that I can look forward to every day.

01:01:52.420 --> 01:01:56.170
So one of the things is like
showering and putting on

01:01:56.170 --> 01:02:00.650
like fragrant body lotions and body oils

01:02:00.650 --> 01:02:02.530
because that's like a mindful thing

01:02:02.530 --> 01:02:05.190
that if you disassociate,
which if you have trauma

01:02:05.190 --> 01:02:08.730
you probably do in some way,
it connects you to your body

01:02:08.730 --> 01:02:11.760
and it also, you know,
connects you to your senses.

01:02:11.760 --> 01:02:14.763
I also drink a lot of like turmeric tea.

01:02:15.940 --> 01:02:19.070
And it's kind of like a
Pavlovian thing where like

01:02:19.070 --> 01:02:22.190
alcohol reminds me of like,

01:02:22.190 --> 01:02:23.880
I'm about to make a call to my dad

01:02:23.880 --> 01:02:25.800
and I need to calm down my nerves.

01:02:25.800 --> 01:02:29.663
But whereas like turmeric
tea or turmeric latte,

01:02:31.750 --> 01:02:34.120
which is just putting
turmeric thing in like water

01:02:34.120 --> 01:02:36.600
and then putting some milk in it,

01:02:36.600 --> 01:02:39.130
it reminds me that I'm
taking care of myself.

01:02:39.130 --> 01:02:40.610
So the more that I do it,

01:02:40.610 --> 01:02:43.950
the more it becomes a reminder to myself

01:02:43.950 --> 01:02:45.500
that I'm taking care of myself.

01:02:47.720 --> 01:02:51.190
I'm working on finding joy.

01:02:51.190 --> 01:02:53.333
I like birds.

01:02:54.260 --> 01:02:57.520
We should've talked more
with Albert about birds.

01:02:57.520 --> 01:02:59.460
So I like feeding them.

01:02:59.460 --> 01:03:04.460
And I have this book by,
what's Sibley's first name?

01:03:05.169 --> 01:03:06.060
- David.

01:03:06.060 --> 01:03:07.623
- David Sibley has a,

01:03:11.410 --> 01:03:14.630
I read Sibley's books on birds

01:03:14.630 --> 01:03:17.130
when I'm feeling like super
stressed and triggered

01:03:17.130 --> 01:03:21.590
because birds are beautiful and resilient.

01:03:21.590 --> 01:03:25.730
So find something that's not
immigration-related, not,

01:03:25.730 --> 01:03:27.640
don't be like, okay, so I'm
gonna take care of myself,

01:03:27.640 --> 01:03:29.380
I'm gonna read Audre Lorde.

01:03:29.380 --> 01:03:32.000
No, like find like birds.

01:03:32.000 --> 01:03:34.670
Before, when I used to be really crazy,

01:03:34.670 --> 01:03:38.380
I used to watch videos of grizzly bears.

01:03:38.380 --> 01:03:42.770
Just like, just take
humans out of the picture.

01:03:42.770 --> 01:03:45.630
Just, just look at a leaf, you know?

01:03:46.680 --> 01:03:48.510
I really recommend that.

01:03:48.510 --> 01:03:49.650
- I second the birds.

01:03:49.650 --> 01:03:51.070
And for those of you wondering

01:03:51.070 --> 01:03:55.100
what tea I was pushing in
my office, it was Tilo,

01:03:55.100 --> 01:03:58.195
which is in English, Linden flower.

01:03:58.195 --> 01:03:59.890
(Karla laughs)

01:03:59.890 --> 01:04:01.180
Yes, it's good calming tea.

01:04:01.180 --> 01:04:02.350
I drink a lot of it,

01:04:02.350 --> 01:04:03.833
that's why it was in my office.

01:04:04.670 --> 01:04:06.810
So another question,

01:04:06.810 --> 01:04:09.160
can you say a bit more
about your writing process,

01:04:09.160 --> 01:04:10.410
where and how you write,

01:04:10.410 --> 01:04:13.160
how you manage moments when
the writing is not flowing?

01:04:16.700 --> 01:04:20.133
- Well, when I have like
a lot of time, I'm like,

01:04:21.070 --> 01:04:22.950
I pretend that a lot of things matter

01:04:22.950 --> 01:04:27.950
such as like inspiration
and I like, you know,

01:04:28.640 --> 01:04:31.380
ultimately, all of it is bullshit

01:04:31.380 --> 01:04:33.230
and when you have, when
you're on deadline,

01:04:33.230 --> 01:04:36.463
all that matters is like that
you sit down and you write.

01:04:38.010 --> 01:04:40.453
What helps is an outline.

01:04:41.360 --> 01:04:43.340
What helps the most is an outline.

01:04:43.340 --> 01:04:47.070
So I'm writing a piece for
The New Yorker right now

01:04:47.070 --> 01:04:50.690
and my editor made me an outline.

01:04:50.690 --> 01:04:53.650
And when I don't have
someone making me an outline,

01:04:53.650 --> 01:04:56.240
my partner, Talia, makes me an outline.

01:04:56.240 --> 01:04:57.740
And when I had the book,

01:04:57.740 --> 01:05:01.740
what I did was I printed out
chapters of the book and I cut,

01:05:01.740 --> 01:05:03.140
Albert, I don't know if you know this,

01:05:03.140 --> 01:05:05.980
I cut out paragraphs, all the paragraphs,

01:05:05.980 --> 01:05:08.600
and sometimes individual sentences

01:05:08.600 --> 01:05:12.140
and then I would tape them on my floor

01:05:12.140 --> 01:05:16.870
in the order in which they were
meant by the gods to appear

01:05:16.870 --> 01:05:20.380
which is actually the way Nabokov's wife

01:05:21.250 --> 01:05:22.680
organized his index cards.

01:05:22.680 --> 01:05:25.150
'Cause he would write
his book in index cards

01:05:25.150 --> 01:05:26.660
and then she reorganized them

01:05:26.660 --> 01:05:28.980
in the way they were supposed to appear.

01:05:28.980 --> 01:05:33.380
'Cause I am not a linear thinker
and so outlines help a lot.

01:05:33.380 --> 01:05:35.880
Talia, my partner, is a fantastic teacher

01:05:35.880 --> 01:05:38.240
and she makes all her
students do outlines.

01:05:38.240 --> 01:05:40.170
So I would say like,

01:05:40.170 --> 01:05:45.170
I believe in like burning
palo santo, candles, skincare,

01:05:45.680 --> 01:05:50.340
moisturizing, doing my
makeup, hair, like matcha,

01:05:50.340 --> 01:05:53.900
all of that until like I'm on a deadline.

01:05:53.900 --> 01:05:57.100
And then I just like put
on a one-piece bathing suit

01:05:57.100 --> 01:05:59.393
and just sit on my bed and just do it.

01:06:01.700 --> 01:06:05.613
- Well, this is a related
question because it's what,

01:06:06.480 --> 01:06:07.800
part of what makes your
writing so distinctive

01:06:07.800 --> 01:06:09.900
is that your voice, right?

01:06:09.900 --> 01:06:11.960
How did you, can you tell us
more, and this is a question,

01:06:11.960 --> 01:06:13.970
can you tell us more about the
process you've gone through

01:06:13.970 --> 01:06:17.743
to develop and refine your
distinctive writerly voice?

01:06:19.870 --> 01:06:21.430
- Yeah.

01:06:21.430 --> 01:06:24.690
I think one of the things you
first start doing is you start

01:06:27.440 --> 01:06:29.120
first you discover things you don't like,

01:06:29.120 --> 01:06:30.270
voices you don't like.

01:06:30.270 --> 01:06:34.210
So Albert knows that I
spent most of my time

01:06:34.210 --> 01:06:38.230
as a grad student being like,
I hate this, I hate this.

01:06:38.230 --> 01:06:41.206
And then I would list
everything that I hated.

01:06:41.206 --> 01:06:43.750
And I wish I had spent more time

01:06:43.750 --> 01:06:46.050
finding out things that I liked.

01:06:46.050 --> 01:06:48.920
So at first I think as a young writer,

01:06:48.920 --> 01:06:51.423
you write against things and you,

01:06:52.570 --> 01:06:54.790
and you shouldn't spend the
rest of your career doing that

01:06:54.790 --> 01:06:56.913
because some people do, some people do,

01:06:58.243 --> 01:07:01.880
just develop a voice that's
just like anti-things

01:07:01.880 --> 01:07:04.050
but then they don't develop
a voice of their own.

01:07:04.050 --> 01:07:08.400
I would say read a lot but
not only like novels or books,

01:07:08.400 --> 01:07:10.720
but like good TV writing.

01:07:10.720 --> 01:07:12.960
Like I was obsessed with "30 Rock."

01:07:12.960 --> 01:07:16.880
I've watched it like
seven straight through.

01:07:16.880 --> 01:07:21.530
And that's like, there's
like, I think there's like,

01:07:21.530 --> 01:07:25.240
there's like, like a record
number of references per minute.

01:07:25.240 --> 01:07:27.890
And like, my writing is
very reference heavy.

01:07:27.890 --> 01:07:29.513
So like that really inspired me.

01:07:32.708 --> 01:07:35.290
I watched a lot of "SNL" that inspired me,

01:07:35.290 --> 01:07:37.630
a lot of rock and roll that inspired me.

01:07:37.630 --> 01:07:39.980
Like anywhere that's good writing,

01:07:39.980 --> 01:07:42.543
like good writing can also be like,

01:07:44.000 --> 01:07:47.360
I remember when I was little,
this is not no longer the case

01:07:47.360 --> 01:07:51.450
but when Vitaminwater
first came out, the copy,

01:07:51.450 --> 01:07:54.760
the back copy of Vitaminwater
used to be pretty witty.

01:07:54.760 --> 01:07:57.847
And I remember being like in
the fifth grade and being like,

01:07:57.847 --> 01:08:00.260
"I wanna write for Vitaminwater one day."

01:08:00.260 --> 01:08:03.060
You know, like wherever
you can find good writing,

01:08:03.060 --> 01:08:06.080
like not just books, but
just let it inspire you.

01:08:06.080 --> 01:08:09.750
And that's where your voice
is going to be like expansive.

01:08:09.750 --> 01:08:14.030
So that's why I tell kids,
like if you are like Latinx

01:08:14.030 --> 01:08:17.800
and you wanna be a writer, don't just read

01:08:17.800 --> 01:08:20.320
like Anzaldúa, you know what I mean?

01:08:20.320 --> 01:08:24.580
Just like read expanse,
watch movies, watch movies,

01:08:24.580 --> 01:08:27.403
but don't just watch like
classics, watch like,

01:08:28.430 --> 01:08:31.413
watch everything, watch
"Paris Is Burning," you know?

01:08:32.330 --> 01:08:36.470
Like watch videos on how
to vogue and realize like

01:08:36.470 --> 01:08:39.783
what different storytelling
looks like, you know?

01:08:41.270 --> 01:08:43.090
Like watching big performances.

01:08:43.090 --> 01:08:44.580
I like watching big performances,

01:08:44.580 --> 01:08:47.950
like, yeah "Homecoming,"
Beyonce's "Homecoming,"

01:08:47.950 --> 01:08:52.070
like Freddie Mercury, like
how is a good performance?

01:08:52.070 --> 01:08:54.220
How is like storytelling done?

01:08:54.220 --> 01:08:58.110
And I think that's how you
pick up like a lot of like

01:08:59.520 --> 01:09:02.650
influences and then when
you sit down on the page,

01:09:02.650 --> 01:09:05.289
you can be you but I'll
tell you the truth,

01:09:05.289 --> 01:09:09.210
when I first started writing,
I used to sit down and I used,

01:09:09.210 --> 01:09:12.130
in order to get me hyped up to write,

01:09:12.130 --> 01:09:12.990
I used to be like,

01:09:12.990 --> 01:09:16.980
okay, such and such essay by Lorrie Moore,

01:09:16.980 --> 01:09:19.530
such and such essay by Joan Didion.

01:09:19.530 --> 01:09:24.110
And I used to try to imitate their voices.

01:09:24.110 --> 01:09:26.060
And I think that was a good exercise

01:09:26.060 --> 01:09:29.850
because then you can see
what like ticks they have,

01:09:29.850 --> 01:09:31.450
what tricks they have.

01:09:31.450 --> 01:09:34.050
And so like, I can do pretty good parodies

01:09:34.050 --> 01:09:36.990
of like Hemingway, Fitzgerald,

01:09:36.990 --> 01:09:39.670
Joan Didion, García Márquez, all of them.

01:09:39.670 --> 01:09:43.950
And like Gertrude Stein, who
is a huge influence of mine,

01:09:43.950 --> 01:09:45.950
partly because I learned to parody her.

01:09:45.950 --> 01:09:49.250
So maybe like try to parody
some of your favorite writers.

01:09:49.250 --> 01:09:52.310
And then I dunno, I dunno,

01:09:52.310 --> 01:09:54.010
you should not take life advice from me

01:09:54.010 --> 01:09:56.780
but that's the stuff that
I did when I was younger.

01:09:56.780 --> 01:09:59.380
- I wanna read the parodies of these,

01:09:59.380 --> 01:10:01.150
of Fitzgerald, Hemingway, Stein.

01:10:01.150 --> 01:10:03.030
I wanna read, that reminds me of,

01:10:03.030 --> 01:10:04.470
Reinaldo Arenas does that for like

01:10:04.470 --> 01:10:06.817
the canon of Cuban
writers in a book called,

01:10:06.817 --> 01:10:08.130
"La Loma del Angel."

01:10:08.130 --> 01:10:10.490
So I would love to read
Karla's version of this

01:10:10.490 --> 01:10:12.410
for the canon of American literature.

01:10:12.410 --> 01:10:15.120
- The Hemingway one is up on McSweeney's.

01:10:15.120 --> 01:10:15.953
- Is it?

01:10:16.920 --> 01:10:17.760
Have I seen it already?

01:10:17.760 --> 01:10:19.130
I've probably seen it.

01:10:19.130 --> 01:10:21.290
Okay, more on that later.

01:10:21.290 --> 01:10:23.410
So the questions are, you
know, jumping around topics,

01:10:23.410 --> 01:10:25.950
that's fine, that's why we have a Q&amp;A.

01:10:25.950 --> 01:10:27.550
So another question asks,

01:10:27.550 --> 01:10:30.440
can you talk about how you
navigated these immigrant spaces

01:10:30.440 --> 01:10:32.850
from your interviews to
your personal interactions,

01:10:32.850 --> 01:10:35.180
as someone who is open and unapologetic

01:10:35.180 --> 01:10:38.146
about mental health and queerness?

01:10:38.146 --> 01:10:39.080
- And queerness?

01:10:39.080 --> 01:10:39.913
- Yes.

01:10:42.890 --> 01:10:47.760
- Okay, so this pixie is new
and I didn't look like this

01:10:47.760 --> 01:10:52.760
when I went on my interviews,
but I was open, I was open.

01:10:55.850 --> 01:10:57.070
So I'll be sincere.

01:10:57.070 --> 01:11:00.223
So first of all for all
of the day laborers,

01:11:01.300 --> 01:11:05.050
they had an organizer who
is named Pedro in the book

01:11:05.050 --> 01:11:07.500
who is gay and openly gay.

01:11:07.500 --> 01:11:09.910
And the way he organized
them was to be like,

01:11:09.910 --> 01:11:11.420
if you were discriminated against,

01:11:11.420 --> 01:11:13.830
here are other groups who
we're discriminated against.

01:11:13.830 --> 01:11:16.400
In the daily laborer worker center,

01:11:16.400 --> 01:11:19.450
there are groups for LGBTQ people,

01:11:19.450 --> 01:11:21.423
there are groups for trans people.

01:11:22.280 --> 01:11:24.890
So they knew that they,

01:11:24.890 --> 01:11:28.050
that they had to be respectful, you know?

01:11:28.050 --> 01:11:30.250
And they all love him.

01:11:30.250 --> 01:11:32.760
It was a complete safe space for me.

01:11:32.760 --> 01:11:36.960
And I had never, I had,

01:11:36.960 --> 01:11:38.750
like with all the people that I spoke to,

01:11:38.750 --> 01:11:41.680
and most of the people that I
spoke to for my book were men,

01:11:41.680 --> 01:11:45.660
I had one experience and it
was with an American citizen

01:11:46.640 --> 01:11:50.070
where I felt uncomfortable as a woman.

01:11:50.070 --> 01:11:51.713
And it was over text.

01:11:53.260 --> 01:11:55.230
Everyone was completely
appropriate with me.

01:11:55.230 --> 01:12:00.230
And so I would, I think
that people really felt,

01:12:00.840 --> 01:12:04.260
something that helped was
that I pitched them the book.

01:12:04.260 --> 01:12:09.170
So the pitch that I gave, like
the publishers to sell them

01:12:09.170 --> 01:12:11.073
was the pitch that I gave immigrants.

01:12:13.200 --> 01:12:15.620
I wanted them to understand
why I was writing

01:12:15.620 --> 01:12:17.937
because a lot of them had
experiences with people from like

01:12:17.937 --> 01:12:21.320
"Univison" and "Telemundo"
coming taking their pictures

01:12:21.320 --> 01:12:23.670
to getting interviews
and then like bouncing

01:12:23.670 --> 01:12:25.880
and then never seeing them again.

01:12:25.880 --> 01:12:27.690
And that's not what I wanted to do.

01:12:27.690 --> 01:12:31.240
Like, I still have some
people that I love who are

01:12:31.240 --> 01:12:34.830
in these communities that I
check in on them and make sure

01:12:34.830 --> 01:12:38.193
that they're doing okay during
COVID, that they, you know,

01:12:39.120 --> 01:12:43.190
I wanna make sure that I,
that they can count on me

01:12:43.190 --> 01:12:44.993
should things become catastrophic.

01:12:48.559 --> 01:12:50.240
I didn't tell them that at the time,

01:12:50.240 --> 01:12:52.737
but I went into the
communities and I was like,

01:12:52.737 --> 01:12:57.417
"Look, so my name's Karla, I'm on DACA.

01:12:57.417 --> 01:12:59.047
"I am a dreamer.

01:12:59.047 --> 01:13:00.317
"I don't identify as a dreamer.

01:13:00.317 --> 01:13:02.417
"Here's why I don't identify as dreamer.

01:13:02.417 --> 01:13:04.117
"My parents are undocumented.

01:13:04.117 --> 01:13:06.267
"I have a complicated
relationship with my parents.

01:13:06.267 --> 01:13:07.610
"Here's why."

01:13:07.610 --> 01:13:09.207
I was like, "This is
the book I wanna write.

01:13:09.207 --> 01:13:11.627
"Here's why books that I
have written about migrants

01:13:11.627 --> 01:13:14.247
"that I've read I feel fall short.

01:13:14.247 --> 01:13:18.137
"Here's why I think we
have not been represented

01:13:18.137 --> 01:13:20.547
"in a way that I'd like
to see us represented.

01:13:20.547 --> 01:13:22.967
"Here's why I think I
can do that differently.

01:13:22.967 --> 01:13:24.777
"Do you trust me to try?"

01:13:27.490 --> 01:13:29.620
I felt like it would
have been condescending

01:13:29.620 --> 01:13:30.487
for me to just be like,

01:13:30.487 --> 01:13:32.820
"Hey, can I interview
you about your life?"

01:13:32.820 --> 01:13:34.670
Without telling them
what I was trying to do

01:13:34.670 --> 01:13:36.200
with their stories.

01:13:36.200 --> 01:13:38.820
And they responded really
well to that, you know?

01:13:38.820 --> 01:13:41.567
They were like, "Yeah, people
think we're alcoholics.

01:13:41.567 --> 01:13:42.500
"Yeah, people."

01:13:42.500 --> 01:13:44.510
And I think like in one of my chapters

01:13:44.510 --> 01:13:47.157
where it's like middle-aged
women who are like,

01:13:47.157 --> 01:13:48.710
"Yeah, we're tired of our husbands.

01:13:48.710 --> 01:13:49.680
'We married too young."

01:13:49.680 --> 01:13:53.477
Like, "I'm having like
a feminist awakening now

01:13:53.477 --> 01:13:54.950
"and I wanna go party."

01:13:54.950 --> 01:13:56.470
Like, that would not have been possible

01:13:56.470 --> 01:13:59.260
if I had not told them
that I was trying to

01:13:59.260 --> 01:14:02.810
change stereotypes and
caricatures of immigrants.

01:14:02.810 --> 01:14:04.077
They would've just been like,

01:14:04.077 --> 01:14:06.730
"Yes, so we are, we are
activists," you know?

01:14:06.730 --> 01:14:07.840
They would have told me the story

01:14:07.840 --> 01:14:09.970
they thought I was trying to get.

01:14:09.970 --> 01:14:13.620
And so one was that,
another thing is that,

01:14:13.620 --> 01:14:16.370
like my Spanish is a very formal,

01:14:16.370 --> 01:14:19.450
like church Spanish, mom and dad Spanish.

01:14:19.450 --> 01:14:22.290
So I reminded them of
their kids and grandkids.

01:14:22.290 --> 01:14:23.790
And during the writing of the book

01:14:23.790 --> 01:14:26.383
I had like pink hair,
purple hair, green hair.

01:14:27.587 --> 01:14:30.677
I had these tattoos and
some of them would be like,

01:14:30.677 --> 01:14:31.747
"You have a lot of tattoos,

01:14:31.747 --> 01:14:33.130
"what do your parents think about that?"

01:14:33.130 --> 01:14:33.963
And I was like,

01:14:33.963 --> 01:14:35.950
"What do you think my
parents think about that?"

01:14:35.950 --> 01:14:38.820
And, you know, it just became like,

01:14:38.820 --> 01:14:40.070
I was my self around them

01:14:40.070 --> 01:14:42.690
and I just reminded them of

01:14:42.690 --> 01:14:45.490
their kids and grandkids who assimilated

01:14:45.490 --> 01:14:48.583
and who they were just shaking
their heads at, you know?

01:14:50.040 --> 01:14:50.873
I looked like them.

01:14:50.873 --> 01:14:52.323
I had their same status.

01:14:55.440 --> 01:14:58.030
The Yale thing didn't
mean anything to them.

01:14:58.030 --> 01:14:59.210
They were proud of it.

01:14:59.210 --> 01:15:01.410
Some of them had never
heard of the school.

01:15:01.410 --> 01:15:06.220
And they were like, they
knew that I worried,

01:15:06.220 --> 01:15:07.270
that I worked hard in order

01:15:07.270 --> 01:15:10.010
so that I could be able to help
my parents out financially.

01:15:10.010 --> 01:15:11.600
And that meant a lot to them.

01:15:11.600 --> 01:15:13.793
The gay stuff, I didn't volunteer it.

01:15:15.920 --> 01:15:17.960
But I said, I talked about my partner.

01:15:17.960 --> 01:15:21.980
It helps that pronouns don't
often come up in Spanish

01:15:21.980 --> 01:15:26.010
but I would be like, "Oh yes,
I have to go to my partner"

01:15:26.010 --> 01:15:27.580
and blah, blah, blah.

01:15:27.580 --> 01:15:31.530
If someone I felt was extremely religious,

01:15:31.530 --> 01:15:36.530
I would say partner and repeat
partner and avoid pronouns.

01:15:37.280 --> 01:15:42.280
But most people, I was just open with

01:15:43.070 --> 01:15:47.930
because I felt like if they
were uncomfortable with me

01:15:47.930 --> 01:15:52.840
being queer and being this,

01:15:52.840 --> 01:15:56.770
like, we wouldn't have the
level of intimacy required

01:15:56.770 --> 01:16:01.180
to form the story that we
needed to form together.

01:16:01.180 --> 01:16:03.133
And I could just find another subject.

01:16:05.520 --> 01:16:09.000
- So last question, oldie
but goodie important

01:16:09.000 --> 01:16:13.053
given the population, the
imagined population audience

01:16:13.053 --> 01:16:15.743
for this conversation is,

01:16:16.670 --> 01:16:19.990
what advice do you have for
aspiring Latinx writers?

01:16:19.990 --> 01:16:23.430
And if you wanna just
wrap up into any of that

01:16:23.430 --> 01:16:25.680
any closing thoughts
you wanna leave us with?

01:16:28.950 --> 01:16:32.583
- I would say, read a lot.

01:16:36.930 --> 01:16:38.740
I would say don't...

01:16:45.220 --> 01:16:47.200
I would say don't listen to what

01:16:47.200 --> 01:16:50.610
Hollywood representations of Latinx people

01:16:51.510 --> 01:16:55.100
have to say about our own representation.

01:16:55.100 --> 01:16:56.970
They're not leading the way here.

01:16:56.970 --> 01:16:59.820
I think we're in a
really interesting moment

01:16:59.820 --> 01:17:03.100
where immigrant artists and Latinx artists

01:17:03.100 --> 01:17:07.440
are trying to do really cool
things with voice and genre.

01:17:07.440 --> 01:17:09.930
Like, I don't know if y'all have read

01:17:09.930 --> 01:17:13.310
Marcelo Hernandez Castillo's
"Children of the Land."

01:17:13.310 --> 01:17:16.633
He's a undocumented author,

01:17:17.470 --> 01:17:20.323
I think on his way to permanent
residency or something.

01:17:23.250 --> 01:17:25.783
He's a poet and he wrote
a really cool book.

01:17:27.150 --> 01:17:29.210
Jenny Zhang was never undocumented,

01:17:29.210 --> 01:17:34.210
but she grew up in Queens,
grew up as an immigrant

01:17:34.500 --> 01:17:39.320
and her book is vulgar and
filthy and wacky and crazy

01:17:40.972 --> 01:17:44.110
and, you know, Salvador
Plascencia's "People of Paper,"

01:17:44.110 --> 01:17:47.693
which I, you know, I
learned from Albert is,

01:17:48.720 --> 01:17:49.873
you know, great book.

01:17:54.420 --> 01:17:56.210
I would say kill your idols,

01:17:56.210 --> 01:17:58.690
you know, commit matricide and patricide,

01:17:58.690 --> 01:18:02.440
like stop looking at Julia
Alvarez and Sandra Cisneros

01:18:02.440 --> 01:18:05.410
and, you know, all of those ladies.

01:18:05.410 --> 01:18:08.180
And stop looking at Gloria
Anzaldúa and all of that,

01:18:08.180 --> 01:18:11.500
like it's past that, it's 2020.

01:18:11.500 --> 01:18:15.310
Like start looking
elsewhere for inspiration.

01:18:15.310 --> 01:18:18.040
Like there are some books that I'm reading

01:18:18.040 --> 01:18:21.830
that are coming out now that
are still describing our skin

01:18:21.830 --> 01:18:24.360
as like (speaks in Spanish) skin

01:18:24.360 --> 01:18:26.930
and are still saying that
our parents are strong

01:18:26.930 --> 01:18:28.720
and can handle anything.

01:18:28.720 --> 01:18:31.560
And that they just like pray
to the (speaks in Spanish)

01:18:31.560 --> 01:18:33.400
and everything will be okay.

01:18:33.400 --> 01:18:35.760
And that is tired.

01:18:35.760 --> 01:18:39.340
And it's this like incestuous relationship

01:18:39.340 --> 01:18:42.130
that the publishing
industry has with Hollywood.

01:18:42.130 --> 01:18:44.210
And that is what has created the backdrop

01:18:44.210 --> 01:18:47.980
for what we think we need to sound like.

01:18:47.980 --> 01:18:50.510
And we don't, we don't.

01:18:50.510 --> 01:18:53.360
And you will receive pushback
for not sounding like that,

01:18:57.007 --> 01:18:59.010
but our canon is young.

01:18:59.010 --> 01:19:04.010
Our Canon is young but
we need to push forward

01:19:04.340 --> 01:19:09.340
because this is like
such an important time

01:19:09.810 --> 01:19:13.200
to really experiment and to
really support each other

01:19:13.200 --> 01:19:17.040
as we create some, you
know, really powerful art

01:19:17.040 --> 01:19:21.190
because if we're creating
art where if you like,

01:19:21.190 --> 01:19:25.500
take a person who's like
been in a bunker for 50 years

01:19:25.500 --> 01:19:27.727
and is like, "Was this
produced in the '90s

01:19:27.727 --> 01:19:29.700
"or was this produced in 2020?"

01:19:29.700 --> 01:19:31.600
And if they can't tell the difference,

01:19:32.520 --> 01:19:34.963
like, you writing it was a waste of time.

01:19:39.090 --> 01:19:39.923
- Well said.

01:19:39.923 --> 01:19:42.183
Well, that's the time we have today.

01:19:43.390 --> 01:19:45.540
MJ, were you gonna jump on
for any closing thoughts?

01:19:45.540 --> 01:19:46.950
I mean, I'm happy.

01:19:46.950 --> 01:19:50.913
Thank you, Karla, obviously
for taking the time to chat.

01:19:52.280 --> 01:19:56.090
Thanks to everyone for logging
on, follow the conversation.

01:19:56.090 --> 01:19:58.130
MJ, you wanna take the mic here?

01:19:58.130 --> 01:20:01.160
- Just wanna thank you both so so so much,

01:20:01.160 --> 01:20:03.300
it was an amazing event.

01:20:03.300 --> 01:20:05.580
For our audiences, thank
you for joining us.

01:20:05.580 --> 01:20:09.360
I am linking our social media on the chat.

01:20:09.360 --> 01:20:10.580
Please follow us.

01:20:10.580 --> 01:20:13.680
There will be many more
events to come, keep in touch.

01:20:13.680 --> 01:20:14.633
So thank you all.

01:20:15.500 --> 01:20:16.993
- Thanks for coming.

01:20:18.750 --> 01:20:20.323
- Take care, everyone.

